\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Down Against European  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2 , Pound Soars \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jamie McGeever \par \uc2 584 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2003\par 22:39\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar weakened against the major European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Wednesday, which were all lifted by massive buying of sterling after a strong suggestion from the Bank of England that U.K. interest rates could be raised soon. \par \par \uc2  The dollar remained well supported against the yen, however, even though there was no overt yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from Japanese monetary authorities, according to dealers. \par \par \uc2  Rather,  the ever-present threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to keep the yen at a competitive level for Japanese exporters, was enough to avert heavier dollar weakness. \par \par \uc2  There was little in the way of fundamental news to give the market direction Wednesday. But in keeping with recent trends, the steep fall in U.S. bond yields - the 10-year note yield fell as low as 4.152% - was closely correlated with the decline in the dollar. \par \par \uc2  This reminded dealers that the return on U.S. assets will remain low, and with the BOE suggesting monetary policy  could soon be tightened, sterling led the charge. \par \par \uc2  "Sterling was extremely well bid...and I think we'll see it go up to $1.6200 in the next few days," said a dealer at a European bank in New York. \par \par \uc2  In late trading in New York, sterling was at $1.6074, more up more than two U.S. cents, or 1.3%, up from $1.5866 late Tuesday in New York. The euro was at $1.1286, up 1% from $1.1169 late Tuesday, and against the yen, was at 131.14 yen, up from 129.89 yen late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 116.21 yen, virtually unchanged from 116.18 yen late Tuesday in New York, but comfortably off the session lows of 115.85 yen. The dollar was weaker against the Swiss franc, however, trading at 1.3781 yen compared with 1.3901 yen. \par \par \uc2  At the end of New York trading, the pound was up over 1% against the dollar from levels 24 hours earlier, while the euro and Swiss franc had both gained around 1%. \par \par \uc2  In London trading Wednesday, the BOE released the minutes from the last monetary policy committee meeting. The minutes contained a line that stated, while interest rates remain on hold, a rate rise "might soon become necessary." \par \par \uc2  And earlier Wednesday, the U.K.'s widely-watched claimant count measure of unemployment fell 6,900 to 930,800 in August, the lowest level since September 1975, National Statistics said. \par \par \uc2  This, coupled with the drop in U.S. yields, helped drive up the euro against the dollar too. \par \par \uc2  "Spread differentials that had favored the U.S. over the euro zone not too long ago are now equal," and could favor the euro in the near term, said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Investors Bank & Trust. \par \par \uc2  Dealers reported heavy euro buying across the board, with pre-placed stop-loss buy orders triggered against the dollar above $1.1250 and $1.1280, and against the yen above 130.80 yen and 131.00 yen. \par \par \uc2  The next target for the euro against the dollar, if it can get above $1.1300, is $1.1350, which is the midpoint of the summer range, said Peter Watkins, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Fortis Bank New York. \par \par \uc2  -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com 