\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slips on profit taking against majors.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 728 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2003\par 20:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comment; changes byline, dateline, previous NEW YORK)  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, Sept 17 (Reuters) - The dollar slipped on Wednesday against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors took advantage of a greenback rally in the previous session, while digesting the implications of a Federal Reserve decision to leave interest rates at a 45-year low.  \par \par \uc2 Trade on was Wednesday was generally quiet, a stark contrast to Tuesday's wild ride when the dollar bolted higher against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pushed up by rallying U.S. stocks and the yen's strength against the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "Really the dollar is in a pull-back mode from yesterday's upmove in yen," said Bob Priore, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk manager at Carr Futures in Chicago. "There may be a little profit taking."  \par \par \uc2 But mixed in with the dollar's climb on Tuesday was the Fed's decision on Tuesday to leave benchmark interest rates at 1 percent. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  indicated at its policy meeting that rates would remain low for the foreseeable future.  \par \par \uc2 In New York midday trading, the euro was up 0.56 percent on the day at $1.1242 . Against the yen, the euro rose 0.37 percent after falling more than 2 percent on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Brian Taylor, head \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at Manufacturers and Traders Bank in Buffalo, New York, said the market had been "overextended" in the euro going into Wednesday's session and had seized on some good buying opportunities at the $1.1150-$1.1175 area.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc, the greenback fell 0.55 percent to 1.3818 francs . Sterling gained a sharp 1.01 percent against the dollar at $1.6036 after minutes from the Bank of England's rate meeting in early September showed some policy-makers starting to look for an interest rate rise.  \par \par \uc2 Gains in sterling versus the dollar have also helped boost other European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the greenback, specifically the euro, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 LOW-RATE BURDEN  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the dollar also reeled from the after-effects of a Federal Reserve policy meeting that kept interest rates at a 45-year low and cautioned against softness in the labor market and the lingering threat of disinflation.  \par \par \uc2 "What has partly hurt the dollar is the fact that the Fed indicated an accommodative policy stance for a sustained period," said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING Capital Markets in New York.  \par \par \uc2 With rates expected to remain at troughs for some time, analysts said investors have temporarily disregarded the U.S. economic rebound story and started looking once again at high-yielders such as Australian, New Zealand and Canadian dollars.  \par \par \uc2 MMS' Simpson reckons that since the U.S. economy may be running out of positive surprises and the soft labor sector continues to weigh on the market, the dollar's upside may be limited until the end of September.  \par \par \uc2 G7 MEETING  \par \par \uc2 Ahead of the Group of Seven meeting this weekend in Dubai, the yen is a major focus in the market as investors puzzle over whether the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was undertaking yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  again.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials said they would act on foreign exchanges if they saw a need, delivering a fresh warning to prevent any tendency in the yen to appreciate - even though this policy may meet criticism from U.S. officials at the G7 meeting.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rose 0.15 percent against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 115.97 yen . Carr's Priore noted sell-stops at 115.90 yen. A breach of that level could pave the way for new losses.  \par \par \uc2 Increasingly, more and more traders were saying that the BoJ was not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to keep the yen weaker against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Taylor, of Manufacturers and Traders Bank, believes the BoJ's hands are tied ahead of the G7 and expects further yen strengthening. "Once we get through 115 yen, there's not much the BoJ can do below that level," he said.  \par \par \uc2 ING's McCarthy also said the BoJ cannot risk taking the heat for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  prior to the G7 gathering.  \par \par \uc2 The BoJ, which acts on behalf of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was unavailable for comment.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers expect yen sentiment to swing ahead of the meeting between equities optimism - after Wednesday's 15-month closing high in the Nikkei stock average - to wariness over Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030918dz9h00148\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
