\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slips as investors digest Fed comment.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez  \par \uc2 614 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2003\par 16:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  (Updates prices, adds comment, changes dateline, byline; previous LONDON)   \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 17 (Reuters)-The dollar faltered on Wednesday against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a generally technically driven market troubled by the Federal Reserve's cautiously optimistic view of the U.S. economy that will keep interest rates low for a long time.   \par \par \uc2  But the greenback rose against the yen amid suspicions the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to weaken the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and protect Japanese exporters' competitive advantage.   \par \par \uc2  "What has partly hurt the dollar is the fact that the Fed indicated an accommodative policy stance," said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING Capital Markets in New York. "People are looking at the interest rates differentials, which are not in favor of the dollar," he said.   \par \par \uc2  In early New York trading, the euro was up 0.37 percent on the day at $1.1215 . Against the Swiss franc, the greenback fell 0.4 percent to 1.3838 francs .   \par \par \uc2  Sterling gained 0.81 percent against the dollar at $1.6003  after minutes from the Bank of England's rate meeting in early September showed some policy-makers starting to look for an interest rate rise.   \par \par \uc2  The Fed on Tuesday kept its rates and biases unchanged. The statement was the same as that issued at the last meeting, apart from a cautious comment on the labor market.   \par \par \uc2  With growth prospects in the United States having been slightly dampened by a stagnant labor market, McCarthy said investors have now switched their focus on yield differentials   \par \par \uc2  "Right now, the commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with their high yields are looking attractive," he added.   \par \par \uc2  G7 MEETING   \par \par \uc2  Ahead of the Group of Seven meeting this weekend in Dubai, the yen continues to be the focus of the market as investors puzzle over whether the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was undertaking yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  again.   \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials said they would act on foreign exchanges if they saw a need, delivering a fresh warning to prevent any tendency in the yen to appreciate - even though this policy may meet criticism from U.S. officials at the G7 meeting.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose 0.20 percent against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 116.38 yen . The euro also climbed against the yen, up 0.55 percent at 130.57 yen .   \par \par \uc2  Philip Capone, a trader at Fortis Bank in New York, said based on the dollar/yen's price action, he believed the BoJ may have been in the market on Wednesday morning, selling the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the 116-116.50 yen level. But no other trader had seen the BoJ sell the yen all session.   \par \par \uc2  The BoJ, which acts on behalf of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was unavailable for comment.   \par \par \uc2  But some traders said the BoJ cannot risk \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  before the G7 event and take the heat from other countries.   \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect yen sentiment to swing ahead of the meeting between equities optimism - after Wednesday's 15-month closing high for the Nikkei stock average - to wariness over Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  "There is generally a more supportive environment for the yen because the Nikkei (stock market index) has been doing well," said Aziz McMahon, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro.   \par \par \uc2  "My own belief is that there will be some sort of opaque statement out of the G7 where they say they all believe in flexible markets but markets need to be stable."   \par \par \uc2  LITTLE IMPACT FROM U.S. HOUSING STARTS   \par \par \uc2  The dollar showed little reaction to U.S. housing starts, which fell 3.8 percent in August. The report indicated that rising mortgage rates are cooling the housing boom. (Additional reporting by Burton Frierson).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030917dz9h002dl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
