\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Fed rates pledge lifts stocks to fresh peaks.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Kaufman  \par \uc2 759 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2003\par 08:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 17 (Reuters) - Japan's Nikkei index hit a 15-month high on Wednesday, leading Asian stock markets higher, while the yen clung to a two-week peak against the dollar after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would keep rates low.  \par \par \uc2 European stock markets were set to push higher in line with strong rallies in U.S. share markets, though early Asian gains had moderated by the end of the session.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign buying helped to drive the Nikkei up one percent to close at 10,990.11, its highest finish since June 2002, although it failed to hold an earlier break through 11,000.  \par \par \uc2 Insurance firms such as Millea Holdings Inc were the day's best performers, reflecting expectations their large share portfolios would benefit from the Nikkei's rally and a move by investors to search the market for laggards in the recent surge. Millea rose over 11 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "Today's jump was sparked by the rise in New York, but this market by itself is so strong," said Kazuyuki Naito, head of sales and trading at UFJ Tsubasa Securities. "Volume and turnover are soaring and the Nikkei has now taken the 11,000 mark."  \par \par \uc2 "I would not be shocked if we were heading towards 14,000 or even 15,000 by the end of the year," said Atsuto Sawakami, fund manager at Sawakami Asset Management.  \par \par \uc2 NTT DoCoMo Inc and other large cap shares gained as well, as did banks such as Mizuho Financial Group Inc and auto makers including Nissan Motor Co DoCoMo gained 3.8 percent, Mizuho jumped 3.2 percent and Nissan added 2.1 percent.  \par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asia-Pacific stock markets outside of Japan in U.S. dollar terms touched a three-year high and was up 0.61 percent at 0210 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Financial bookmakers in London expected Britain's FTSE 100 to open 20 points firmer, Germany's DAX index to open up 25 points and France's CAC-40 to start 23 points higher.  \par \par \uc2 FED TO HOLD DOWN RATES  \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve officials opted on Tuesday to keep U.S. interest rates at 45-year lows, and said rates would stay down for a long time to lift an economy that is losing jobs. That boosted the Dow Jones Industrial Average 1.25 percent and the Nasdaq Composite Index 2.25 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The gains in Japanese stocks helped the yen hold on to gains from U.S. trade, supported by rumours that European fund operators were rebalancing portfolios in favour of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Traders said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may try to avoid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  before this weekend's Group of Seven meeting in Dubai, amid international concerns about manipulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but could feel forced to if the dollar fell below 116 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar stood at 116.11 yen close to where it was in late New York trade on Tuesday. The euro was quoted weaker at $1.1156 and steady at 129.57 yen.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasuries were unchanged in Asian trade, while Japan government bonds firmed despite the Nikkei's rise on expectations Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi would win a weekend party leadership vote.  \par \par \uc2 FUND MANAGERS BULLISH  \par \par \uc2 Lending support to stocks was a survey of global fund managers by Merrill Lynch, which on Tuesday showed asset managers turning aggressively bullish about prospects for the world economy and corporate profits.  \par \par \uc2 "It is the most optimistic survey I've seen in a long time," said Merrill Lynch's chief investment strategist, David Bowers.  \par \par \uc2 Japan is increasingly favoured by fund managers, who in September regarded it as the second-most promising area for growth after emerging markets, Merrill said.  \par \par \uc2 Technology stocks were leading gains across Asia on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co (TSMC), the world's largest contract chip maker, rose 4.4 percent, helping the Taipei market gain 0.8 percent. Its Singapore rival, Chartered Semiconductor, jumped 6.8 percent.  \par \par \uc2 South Korean shares tracked the U.S. markets, with technology shares such as Samsung Electronics Co Ltd leading the Korea Composite Stock Price Index (KOSPI) up 0.39 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's weakness put pressure on gold. Spot gold was trading at $372.90 an ounce, down from New York's close of $373.55.  \par \par \uc2 Oil recovered some ground lost in New York, but volumes were light ahead of the release of weekly U.S. petroleum data. Front-month October crude was up 10 cents at $27.66 per barrel after falling 58 cents on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030917dz9h001bl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
