\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Asian  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  reserves keep rising, top $1.66 trillion.  \b0\par\par\uc2 544 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2003\par 07:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 17 (Reuters) - Asian ex-Pacific \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are rising strongly, reflecting ongoing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the region's central banks, according to official \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  data.  \par \par \uc2 South Korea said on Wednesday its reserves rose $1.9 billion in just the first 15 days of September, pushing up its total \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings to $138.1 billion. The country's reserves were less than $4 billion in late 1997.  \par \par \uc2 The steady build-up of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, mostly in U.S. dollars, reflects steady \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the region's monetary authorities to stall \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation due to strong portfolio inflows and rising export receipts.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. and European policymakers have criticised this \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying it makes their exports less competitive and leads to job losses.  \par \par \uc2 The issue will probably be a hot topic at this weekend's meeting of the Group of Seven industrialised countries in Dubai.  \par \par \uc2 Asia ex-Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves rose by $11.1 billion in June and by $14.7 billion in July, according to Lehman Brothers. Much of this money is reinvested in U.S. Treasuries, capital flow data from the Federal Reserve shows.  \par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  make up a combined 39.2 percent of the Federal Reserve's trade-weighted index of the dollar, so any move to allow Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate would have a significant impact on the dollar, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 Foreigners' net purchases of U.S. Treasury bonds and notes totaled $44.7 billion in July, up from $44.1 billion in June, according to Treasury Department data released on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 The biggest holder of U.S. Treasury bonds and notes was Japan, with holdings of $443.8 billion in July, up from $441.6 billion the prior month.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has recently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to check the appreciation of the yen in order to protect Japanese exporters' competitiveness as Japan's economy recovers.  \par \par \uc2 China, whose policy of keeping the yuan tightly pegged has been widely criticised by U.S. lawmakers, is the third largest owner of U.S. Treasuries, behind Britain, with holdings of $126.1 billion in July, a rise from $122.5 billion in June.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign accounts made net purchases in July of long term U.S. agency bonds of $11.8 billion, up from $7.2 billion net purchases in June.  \par \par \uc2 Foreigners' net purchases of U.S. corporate bonds were $26.4 billion in July, up from net purchases of $22.9 billion in June.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts said that for the foreseeable future they expect that both China and Japan will continue to make hefty purchases of Treasuries in order to keep their respective   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from appreciating against the dollar. Country\par       \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves in U.S. dollars\par   Japan                  $555.1 billion\par   China                   356.5\par   Taiwan                  185.7\par   South Korea             138.1\par   Hong Kong               111.8\par   Singapore                87.8\par   India                    87.4\par   Malaysia                 38.7\par   Thailand                 39.0\par   Indonesia                33.6\par   Philippines              16.2\par   Pakistan                 11.1\par   Bangladesh                2.6 Total:                $1,663.5\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Note: The United States holds $80.6 billion in reserve assets, Germany has $89.7 billion: source IMF (see  \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.imf.org/external/np/sta/ir/colist.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.imf.org/external/np/sta/ir/colist.htm}}}\uc2 \f2 ).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030917dz9h0013z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
