\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA MARKETS-Fed rates pledge lifts stocks to fresh peaks.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Kaufman  \par \uc2 753 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 September 2003\par 05:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Sept 17 (Reuters) - Asian stocks jumped on Wednesday, hitting a 15-month high in Tokyo and a three-year peak elsewhere, and the yen held near a two-week high against the dollar after the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said it would keep rates low.  \par \par \uc2 Foreign buying helped to drive Tokyo's Nikkei index up 1.41 percent in morning trade to 11,040.66, its highest since June 2002. The broader TOPIX index was up 1.54 percent from a 15-month closing high hit on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "It's not at all surprising the Nikkei broke through 11,000 - there are so many companies in Japan that have broken free of recession-like conditions and are attractive investments," said Atsuto Sawakami, fund manager at Sawakami Asset Management.  \par \par \uc2 "I would not be shocked if we were heading towards 14,000 or even 15,000 by the end of the year."  \par \par \uc2 Banks such as Mizuho Financial Group Inc and auto makers such as Nissan Motor Co continued to lead the charge after heady gains on Tuesday. Mizuho rose 10 percent and Nissan gained 2.3 percent.  \par \par \uc2 MSCI's broadest index of Asian stock market activity outside of Japan touched a three-year high and was up 0.8 percent at 0240 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve officials opted on Tuesday to keep U.S. interest rates at 45-year lows, and said rates would stay down for a long time to lift an economy that is losing jobs. That boosted the Dow Jones Industrial Average 1.25 percent and the Nasdaq Composite Index 2.25 percent.  \par \par \uc2 FUND MANAGERS BULLISH  \par \par \uc2 Lending support to stocks was a survey of global fund managers by Merrill Lynch, which showed asset managers turning aggressively bullish about prospects for the world economy and corporate profits.  \par \par \uc2 "It is the most optimistic survey I've seen in a long time," said Merrill Lynch's chief investment strategist, David Bowers.  \par \par \uc2 Japan is increasingly favoured by fund managers, who in September regarded it as the second-most promising area for growth after emerging markets, Merrill said.  \par \par \uc2 The yen held on to gains from U.S. trade, supported by rumours that European fund operators were rebalancing portfolios in favour of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may try to avoid \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  before this weekend's Group of Seven meeting in Dubai amid international concerns about manipulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Still, most dealers were cautious about selling the dollar below 116 yen, fearing the Bank of Japan would step in to stem the yen's advance beyond that level.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think operators will want to sell the dollar below 116 yen, but its downswing could pick up pace if it breaks through the previous low of 115.75 yen," said Hideaki Furumaya, head of the corporate desk at Trust and Custody Services Bank.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar at 115.75 yen was the low hit on September 4 and the level where the BOJ was suspected to have resumed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after refraining from such action in August.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar stood at 116.28 yen compared with 116.15 yen in late New York on Tuesday. The euro was quoted weaker at $1.1162 and steady at 129.90 yen.  \par \par \uc2 TECHS JUMP  \par \par \uc2 Technology stocks were leading gains across Asia.  \par \par \uc2 Taiwan investors bought United Microelectronics Corp (UMC), the market's most active share, leading the technology sub-index 1.90 percent higher. UMC rival Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co (TSMC), the world's largest contract chip maker, was up over two percent.  \par \par \uc2 South Korean shares tracked the U.S. markets, with technology shares such as Samsung Electronics Co Ltd and LG Electronics Co leading the Korea Composite Stock Price Index (KOSPI) up 0.88 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Blue chips gains led Singapore's Straits Times Index and Hong Kong's Hang Seng Index 1.28 percent and 0.82 percent higher respectively, as worries over SARS and the economy diminished.  \par \par \uc2 Australian shares were led higher by globally exposed companies, pushing the S&P/ASX 200 index up 0.7 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's weakness put pressure on gold. Spot gold was trading at $373.00 an ounce, down from New York's close of $373.55.  \par \par \uc2 Oil recovered some ground lost in New York, but volumes were light ahead of the release of weekly U.S. petroleum data. Front-month October crude was up 13 cents at $27.69 per barrel after falling 58 cents on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030917dz9h000ux\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
