\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW: Ex-MOF Utsumi Expects G7 OK On Japan  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Steps \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ron Harui \par \uc2 474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2003\par 11:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Top finance officials of the Group of Seven industrialized nations are unlikely to call on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to temper its massive \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when they meet this weekend in Dubai, a former Japanese vice minister for international affairs said Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  "I think it's very improbable that one country among the G7 is singled out," Makoto Utsumi, who is now president of the think tank Japan Center for International Finance, told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. \par \par \uc2  Japan has received increased criticism from U.S. manufacturers and politicians over its aggressive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent an excessive yen rise from stamping out the nation's export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has acknowledged selling a record Y9 trillion in the first seven months of the year via the Bank of Japan, and is suspected to have sold billions more yen this month to halt a strengthening in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Utsumi said the G7 is expected to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and is likely to reaffirm its stance that \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  should reflect economic fundamentals and that member nations will cooperate as needed if there are excessive fluctuations. \par \par \uc2  Japan's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  are "psychologically very important" in its battle with speculators as the Japanese authorities are keeping these players guessing about its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  tactics, Utsumi said. \par \par \uc2  Echoing his former colleagues at the Finance Ministry, Utsumi said the ministry's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are aimed at countering speculative forces behind the yen's moves against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as those that drove the dollar down to a 27-month low of Y115.10 on May 19, 2003. \par \par \uc2  At 0850 GMT, the dollar was at Y116.49. \par \par \uc2  Japanese authorities are likely to continue defending the psychologically and technically key level of Y115, although the dollar's fall below that level wouldn't immediately harm the Japanese economy, Utsumi said. \par \par \uc2  "It would be very difficult for (the dollar), at even Y1 or Y2 below Y115, to hurt Japan's economy," he said, pointing out that the nation's economy managed to grow when the dollar rebounded into the Y105-Y110 range in early 1996 after it fell to an all-time low of around Y79.85 on April 19, 1995. \par \par \uc2  Utsumi said he expects the dollar to trade in a range of Y115-Y120 for the rest of 2003, with a downward bias given growing signs of economic recovery in Japan. \par \par \uc2  But he added there is a slim possibility of the dollar falling to Y110 if the U.S.'s huge current-account deficit widens further and foreign capital inflows into the U.S. slow significantly. \par \par \uc2  -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2942; ron.harui@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Kirsty Mackenzie \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030916dz9g000lb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
