\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Like It Or Not, Asia Flows Plug US Deficits \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jamie McGeever \par \uc2 886 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2003\par 20:13\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- They certainly don't like it, but they might just have to get used to it. \par \par \uc2  U.S. lawmakers and manufacturers looking for a scapegoat to blame for the slump in U.S. jobs have settled upon Asian central banks. They accuse them of either actively spending in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  artificially weak or tightly controlling their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at the expense of U.S. manufacturing jobs. \par \par \uc2  Recent capital flows data and the upcoming weekend meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven leading industrialized nations brings the prickly issue of official \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies into even sharper focus. \par \par \uc2  Data from the U.S. Commerce Department Monday showed  the U.S. current account - the broadest measure of the country's goods and services trade with the rest of the world - ran a $138.7 billion deficit in the second quarter. As a proportion of gross domestic product, that's 5.14%. \par \par \uc2  Much of this gap was with Asia, where cheaper labor costs - and some would claim, cheaper \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - result in cheaper goods on global markets. \par \par \uc2  According to independent researchers 4Cast, 41% of this second quarter deficit, or $57 billion, was funded by official \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  purchases of U.S. securities. Of that, $42 billion came from Asian central banks. Analysts say this reflects \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , under which Asian central banks - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's is at the top of the pack - buy dollars in the markets and keep those dollars in Treasurys and other U.S. fixed-income securities. \par \par \uc2  Throw on top of these figures other flows data and anecdotal evidence and the signs suggest that official Asian purchases of U.S. assets won't let up any time soon, despite increasing pressure from the U.S., and more vocally now, Europe. \par \par \uc2  "Will Asian officials take heed?" asked Marcel Kasumovich, global head of G10 strategy at Merrill Lynch in New York. "It's a tough call, a very tough call. (But) there's little indication of Asian central banks moving to more flexible (exchange rate) regimes." \par \par \uc2  The G7 pow-wow in Dubai on Saturday will center on just that, or more specifically the Chinese yuan's peg to the dollar and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's overt and aggressive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  drives. \par \par \uc2  Asia and Africa run a huge current account surplus with the U.S. of an annualized $240 billion, of which China's share is in excess of $100 billion, and Japan's around $69 billion. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par      Dollar Glass Half Full Or Half Empty?\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Of the outstanding $1.239 trillion of U.S. government securities, Asia\par holds around $819 billion, the lion's share of which is held by China\par and Japan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The strength of dollar buying suggests Asian central banks are seeing\par strong inflows into their regions," wrote Mansoor Mohi-uddin, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par strategist at UBS, in a research note Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par As Michael Woolfolk, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Bank of New York in New\par York points out, official Chinese and Japanese buying of U.S. securities\par has been a huge factor behind the net $253.7 billion foreign inflow into\par Treasuries and agency bonds in the March-July period.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par So far this year, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has\par officially totaled just under $80 billion. But market participants say\par this has increased by at least $15 billion in September alone. Japan's\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will release official figures for September at the\par end of the month.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And other Asian central banks, including those of South Korea and\par Taiwan, have also been active in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to keep their\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from strengthening too much against the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The latest weekly figures from the Federal Reserve show that in the\par five days to Sept. 10, foreign central banks were net buyers of\par Treasurys and agencies for the sixth straight week, buying a net $8\par billion. This is up from $4 billion the previous week, which pushed the\par four-week moving average up to $5.4 billion.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And Tuesday, the U.S. Treasury released data for the month of July\par that show net foreign portfolio investment into the U.S. totaled $73.8\par billion in that period, down from a revised $91.1 billion net inflow in\par July, but still up on $70.8 billion the same month last year.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This decline from the previous month will weigh on the dollar, say\par analysts at JP Morgan.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "We believe this decline in total net portfolio flows into the U.S.\par continued in recent weeks and will persist in coming months," they wrote\par in a note Tuesday. "Thus, any data-related bounce in the dollar should\par be short-lived."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar's low-yield status will likely keep a lid on capital flows\par into the U.S., making it more difficult to fund the current-account\par deficit in the medium-term.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But Woolfolk at Bank of New York disagrees, saying the persistently\par high levels of investment into the U.S. "remains sufficient to keep the\par dollar stable."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (Jamie McGeever, who covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in New York, has\par also reported on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in Rio de Janeiro,\par London and Madrid.)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096;\par jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030916dz9g009kh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
