\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar extends gains after Fed holds rates steady.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 541 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2003\par 21:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, Sept 16 (Reuters) - The dollar vacillated, then extended its gains against the euro on Tuesday after the Federal Reserve announced, as expected, no change in U.S. interest rates, saying rates could remain low for "a considerable period."  \par \par \uc2 The Fed left its overnight lending rate at a 45-year low of 1 percent. Most economists had anticipated that decision.  \par \par \uc2 In an accompanying statement, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said: "The Committee judges that on balance the risk of inflation becoming undesirably low remains the predominant concern for the foreseeable future."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the perceived risk of deflation weighed on debt yields, putting light pressure on the dollar as well, but that pressure soon dissipated. The dollar remained sharply higher against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Earlier in the global day, the yen shot higher against a host of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the dollar, after the benchmark Nikkei stock index turned in a strong performance overnight after the Bank of Japan raised its view on the world's second-largest economy in its monthly report.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback drew additional support from the sharp rally in the yen, which weighed heavily on European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The euro fell 2 percent against the yen and 1 percent against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 After the Fed announcement, the euro was down 1.07 percent on the day at $1.1163 . Against the Swiss franc, the greenback was up 0.7 percent to 1.3897 francs . Sterling was down 0.9 percent against the dollar at $1.5860 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar declined 1.1 percent against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 116.13 yen . The euro fell steeply against the yen, down 2.2 percent at 129.61 yen .  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar is being helped by the yen's rise across the board," said Steven Englander, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for North America at Barclay's Capital.  \par \par \uc2 "When the yen strengthens across the board, the euro normally weakens against the dollar," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the yen's rising influence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is being driven by expectations that Japan will be one of the biggest winners in a global economic recovery. Given its huge trade surplus with most countries, Japan is well-positioned to benefit from a global upswing in the industrial production cycle.  \par \par \uc2 Markets were also speculating \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may hold off \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen gains ahead of a Group of Seven ministerial meeting this weekend. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be a key issue during the meeting.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. CONSUMER PRICE DATA  \par \par \uc2 Weaker-than-expected consumer price numbers for August hardly made a dent on the dollar's overall performance. August CPI data showed an increase of 0.3 percent. This was slightly lower than market expectations of 0.4 percent.  \par \par \uc2 But excluding food and energy costs, the "core" CPI was up 0.1 percent last month, compared with a rise of 0.2 percent in July.  \par \par \uc2 "For the United States, the disinflationary trend continues," said Sal Guateri, senior economist at BMO Financial Group in Toronto. "This portrays an economy operating with a lot of slack, i.e., unused capacity. For the dollar, it is a near-term negative because the Fed will keep rates low for a long time," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030916dz9g003os\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
