\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar up on firm data, awaiting FOMC.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 594 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2003\par 19:51\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comment, changes byline, changes dateline; previous NEW YORK)  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, Sept 16 (Reuters) - The dollar climbed on Tuesday, supported by firm U.S. economic data, as markets waited for what many thought would be an upgraded assessment of the U.S. economy from the Federal Reserve.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback took additional support from a sharp rally in the yen, which weighed heavily on European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The euro fell 2 percent against the yen and 1 percent against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Fed's policy-setting committee met on Tuesday, but few expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to move rates from the current 45-year low of 1 percent. Instead, all eyes are on the Fed's economic assessment within the rate announcement due at 2:15 p.m. EDT.  \par \par \uc2 Most analysts expect the Fed will not deviate much from its August statement - splitting its risk assessment between growth and inflation. They also anticipate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to maintain a loose monetary policy stance for a considerable period.  \par \par \uc2 "I think they'll leave policy unchanged and talk about balance (of) risks," said Michael Malpede, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Refco. "I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are more focused on what's happening with the euro/yen cross."  \par \par \uc2 The yen shot higher against a host of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the dollar, after the benchmark Nikkei stock index turned in a strong performance overnight, sparked after the Bank of Japan raised its view on the world's second-largest economy in its monthly report.  \par \par \uc2 At midday in New York, the euro was down 1.05 percent at $1.1164 . Against the Swiss franc, the greenback rose 0.86 percent to 1.3915 francs . Sterling was down 0.79 percent against the dollar at $1.5881 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar declined 1.02 percent against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to 116.21 yen . The euro fell steeply as well against the yen, down 2.08 percent at 129.78 yen .  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar is being helped by the yen's rise across the board," said Steven Englander, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for North America at Barclay's Capital.  \par \par \uc2 "When the yen strengthens across the board, the euro normally weakens against the dollar," he said, adding that the yen has also surged against most European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the yen's rising influence in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is being driven by expectations that Japan will be one of the biggest winners in a global economic recovery. Given its huge trade surplus with most countries, Japan is well-positioned to benefit from a global upswing in the industrial production cycle.  \par \par \uc2 Markets were also speculating \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may hold off \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem yen gains ahead of a Group of Seven ministerial meeting this weekend. \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be a key issue during the meeting.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. CONSUMER PRICE DATA  \par \par \uc2 Weaker-than-expected consumer price numbers for August hardly made a dent on the dollar's overall performance. August CPI data showed an increase of 0.3 percent. This was slightly lower than market expectations of 0.4 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Excluding food and energy costs, the "core" CPI was up 0.1 percent last month, compared with a rise of 0.2 percent in July.  \par \par \uc2 "For the United States, the disinflationary trend continues," said Sal Guateri, senior economist at BMO Financial Group in Toronto.  \par \par \uc2 "This portrays an economy operating with a lot of slack, i.e., unused capacity. For the dollar, it is a near-term negative because the Fed will keep rates low for a long time," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030916dz9g003k4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
