\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar shines ahead of Fed - yen driving markets.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez  \par \uc2 647 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2003\par 17:58\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 16 (Reuters) - The dollar climbed against most major counterparts on Tuesday, helped indirectly by the yen's rally against some European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on growing optimism about the Japanese economy, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 Expectations that the Federal Reserve will upgrade its assessment of the U.S. economy following firmer economic data in the past few months triggered some buying in the greenback earlier.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , however, fell against the yen on the Nikkei's strong performance overnight after the Bank of Japan raised its view on the world's second largest economy in its monthly report.  \par \par \uc2 Midday in New York, the euro was sharply down 0.95 percent at $1.1178 . Against the Swiss franc, the greenback rose 0.89 percent to 1.3918 francs . Sterling was down 0.81 percent against the dollar at $1.5878 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar declined 0.86 percent against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 116.41 yen . The euro fell steeply as well against the yen, down 1.79 percent at 130.17 yen .  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar is being helped by the yen's rise across the board," said Steven Englander, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist for North America at Barclay's Capital.  \par \par \uc2 "When the yen strengthens across the board, the euro normally weakens against the dollar," he said, adding that the yen has also surged against most European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Sterling  and the Swiss franc           were down 1.67 percent and and\par 1.8 percent respectively against the yen.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts say the yen's rising influence in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market is being driven by expectations that Japan will be one of the biggest winners in a global economic recovery. Given its huge trade surplus with most countries, Japan is well-positioned to benefit from a global upswing in the industrial production cycle.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets were also speculating \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may hold off \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem yen gains ahead of a Group of Seven (G7) ministerial meeting this weekend. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be a key issue during the meeting.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FED MEETING  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets were also focusing on the upcoming Fed policy meeting on Tuesday, with the dollar drawing support from expectations that the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will raise its evaluation of the U.S. economy by a notch.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market is pricing in expectations that the Fed this afternoon will probably give a strong outlook on the U.S. economy. That has been boosting the dollar," said Kathy Lien, chief fundamental strategist at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Capital Markets in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the Fed is expected to hold interest rates unchanged currently at 1 percent, clues as to how the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  views U.S. growth prospects and the sluggish labor market could prove catalysts for dollar moves.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most analysts, however, expect the Fed will not deviate much from its August statement on Tuesday - splitting its risk assessment between growth and inflation. They also anticipate the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to maintain a loose monetary policy stance for a considerable period.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A Fed announcement is expected at 2:15 p.m. EDT (1815 GMT).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. CONSUMER PRICE DATA  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Weaker-than-expected consumer price numbers for August hardly made a dent on the dollar's overall performance. August CPI data showed an increase of 0.3 percent. This was slightly lower than market expectations of 0.4 percent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Excluding food and energy costs, the "core" CPI was up 0.1 percent last month, compared to a rise of 0.2 percent in July.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "For the United States, the disinflationary trend continues," said Sal Guateri, senior economist, BMO Financial Group in Toronto.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This portrays an economy operating with a lot of slack, i.e., unused capacity. For the dollar, it is a near-term negative because the Fed will keep rates low for a long time," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Additional reporting by Daniel Bases in New York).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030916dz9g0036b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
