\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar weak, little impact from Shiokawa.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Yonggi Kang  \par \uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2003\par 05:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 16 (Reuters) - The dollar fell by midday on Tuesday, undermined by U.S. data that raised doubts about the strength of the current economic recovery in the United States.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese Finance Minister Masajuro Shiokawa's decision to cancel his trip to Dubai where a Group of Seven meeting is scheduled later this week and comments suggesting that he may not stay at his post had little impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's losses were relatively subdued, with many market players waiting to see how the Federal Reserve - widely expected to keep rates unchanged at its policy meeting on Tuesday - will assess the state of the U.S. economy.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the greenback found some support due to wariness about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 As of 0246 GMT, the dollar was at 117.16/21 yen down from around 117.50 yen in late U.S. trade. Traders said gains in Japanese share prices also helped the yen.  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei share average ended the morning up 0.42 percent at 10,758.33.  \par \par \uc2 The euro rose to $1.1302/05 from late U.S. levels around $1.1285. The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 132.40/51 yen compared with 132.60 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Shiokawa, who has been sidelined due to illness, said it was not urgent that he attend the G7 meeting given that he shared the same views as U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow on international \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes.  \par \par \uc2 The finance minister also said he had no plan to resign although his recent illness might make it difficult for him to stay on in his job.  \par \par \uc2 "It will take me time to build up my stamina," he told his first news conference following hospitalisation for gall bladder inflammation. "It could be difficult to carry on."  \par \par \uc2 He added that he would leave the decision on whether he continues in his post up to Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi.  \par \par \uc2 Koizumi is expected to reshuffle his cabinet next week assuming he wins an election for party leader on September 20 and retains his prime ministership.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market showed muted reaction to Shiokawa's remarks.  \par \par \uc2 "We're not seeing any significant moves," said Gen Kawabe, treasury department manager at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking.  \par \par \uc2 "Many market players see Koizumi's reelection as a yen positive factor but Shiokawa's resignation would not lead to thinking that his structural reforms will be delayed."  \par \par \uc2 EYES ON FED  \par \par \uc2 Hopes for a strong recovery, which led the dollar's rally from April to June, are now facing a reality check, as recent data showed lethargic U.S. labour markets hardly benefitted from a recent upturn in the economy.  \par \par \uc2 Concerns over the U.S. economy pushed down the dollar versus the Australian dollar, which rose to 66.59/64 U.S. cents from around 66.50 and threatened to mark a fresh seven-week high.  \par \par \uc2 For now, though, the market is focusing on the Fed's policy-setting meeting.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to leave official interest rates unchanged, but Wall Street will be closely watching the Fed's view of recent economic growth, which some believe may not last into next year.  \par \par \uc2 The Federal Open Market Committee, which sets interest rates, will release a statement at 1815 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials, meanwhile, reiterated their intention to curb the yen's strengthening.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, Zembei Mizoguchi, said on Tuesday that any action Japan takes in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is aimed at preventing volatility and overshooting.  \par \par \uc2 Mizoguchi also shrugged off speculation that other Group of Seven countries may urge \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at their weekend summit in Dubai, by saying he saw no need to explain Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  position further at the meeting.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030916dz9g0018h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
