\par\par \b \uc2 Dlr eases as doubts emerge over U.S. recovery.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano  \par \uc2 487 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 September 2003\par 04:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 16 (Reuters) - The dollar fell on Tuesday, undermined by U.S. data that raised doubts about the strength of the current economic recovery in the United States.  \par \par \uc2 Still, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's losses were relatively small, with many market players waiting to see how the Federal Reserve - widely expected to keep rates unchanged at its policy meeting on Tuesday - will assess the state of the U.S. economy.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the greenback found some support due to wariness about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 As of 0146 GMT, the dollar was at 117.27/29 yen down from around 117.50 yen in late U.S. trade. Traders said early gains in Japanese share prices also helped the yen.  \par \par \uc2 The euro rose to $1.1305/10 from late U.S. levels around $1.1285. The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 132.60/65 yen compared with 132.60 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Optimism remains about the U.S. economy, based on a scenario for a jobless recovery, such as one the economy experienced in the early 90s.  \par \par \uc2 But hopes for a strong recovery, which led the dollar's rally from April to June, are now facing a reality check, as recent data showed lethargic U.S. labour markets hardly benefitted from a recent upturn in the economy.  \par \par \uc2 "With employment not recoverying, consumption is unlikely to grow," said Hiroshi Yokotani, economist at Tokio Marine Asset Management.  \par \par \uc2 Industrial production released on Monday crept up 0.1 percent in August, weaker than forecasts. This came after a surprise softening in the University of Michigan's consumer sentiment index on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "The latest data also shows companies are not revising up their production plans despite growth of five percent in the July-September quarter," Yokotani said.  \par \par \uc2 Concerns over the U.S. economy pushed down the dollar versus the Australian dollar, which rose to 66.61/66 U.S. cents from around 66.50 and threatened to mark a fresh seven-week high.  \par \par \uc2 For now, though, the market is focusing on the Fed's policy-setting meeting later in the day.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to leave official interest rates unchanged, but Wall Street will be closely watching the Fed's view of recent economic growth, which some believe may not last into next year.  \par \par \uc2 The Federal Open Market Committee, which sets interest rates, will release a statement at 1815 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials, meanwhile, reiterated their intention to curb the yen's strengthening.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief, Zembei Mizoguchi, said on Tuesday that any actions Japan takes in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market are aimed at preventing volatility and overshooting.  \par \par \uc2 Mizoguchi also shrugged off speculation that other Group of Seven countries may urge \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at their weekend summit in Dubai, by saying he saw no need to explain Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  position further at the meeting.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030916dz9g000bw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
