\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Closes Slightly Higher Ahead of FOMC Meeting \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Barkley \par \uc2 971 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2003\par 23:13\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  The dollar failed to stage much of a comeback against the euro Monday ahead of a Federal Reserve rate-setting meeting, hurt by indications that U.S. industrial production is growing at a slower pace than anticipated. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Swedish krona fell sharply following a vote by Swedes to resoundingly reject membership of European Monetary Union, although it recouped some of its losses. Late Monday in New York, the euro was trading at 9.1439 kronor, sharply higher than 9.0760 kronor late Friday but well below the 9.20 kronor it had reached on the back of the rejection. \par \par \uc2  Observers said the result illustrates the gap between the goals and visions of the European Union's political and business elite on one hand and the mindset of their constituents on the other. \par \par \uc2  In the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar was able to extend its gains modestly against the yen, as investors remain wary of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the seemingly heavy presence of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which acts on behalf of the government, last week. Japan has been a dominant force in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets this year as it seeks to stop the yen from hitting 115 yen, a level though to be the pain threshold for many exporters. \par \par \uc2  Trading was subdued, however, amid a national holiday in Japan and ahead of the Federal Reserve Open Market Committee's interest-rate decision on Tuesday. While benchmark rates are almost certain to stay on hold at 1%,  the focus is on what the Fed will say about the risks of inflation or disinflation. \par \par \uc2  "The market probably expects the Fed to say `the economy's great, but we're worried about unemployment, and that the fear of deflation is worse than that of inflation,'" said Mark Thome, vice president of Treasury at Fortis Bank New York. But he added the meeting is "so far off the radar screen" that any comments about the economy being better or worse than expected could move the markets. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. recovery remains the big story, but dollar bulls have lost a lot of their enthusiasm since the release of disappointing August payroll data, which showed the economy continued to shed jobs. \par \par \uc2  Against that backdrop, the unwieldy U.S. current-account deficit remains a weight on the dollar. The Commerce Department said the current-account gap held largely steady in the second quarter from an upwardly revised $138.7 billion in the first quarter. The deficit is running at around 5.1% of gross domestic product, a level flagged as a danger zone by many economists. \par \par \uc2  Late Monday in New York, the dollar was trading around 117.50 yen, up from 117.33 yen late Friday in New York. The euro was at $1.1283, little changed from $1.1288 late Friday. Sterling was at $1.6011, down from $1.6061. \par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies are still the focus of many  traders, but they appear less willing to test the resolve of Japanese authorities. That, along with thin volume, kept the dollar hovering around 117.50 yen for much of the day. \par \par \uc2  While \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators in the week ended Tuesday added to their net long yen position, the largest in four years, analysts believe many of them have since withdrawn in the face of as much as $10 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past week. \par \par \uc2  "It seems like the speculative community definitely got overextended, and I think they've been pared back, which allows some of the pressure [for a stronger yen] to be taken off," said Lauren Germain, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Banc of American Securities in New York. \par \par \uc2  The latest positioning data from the International Monetary Market on the Chicago \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -futures exchange show traders held net long yen positions of 52,845 contracts in the week ended Tuesday, up from 46,955 the week before. Last week's figure was the second highest on record. Extreme positioning can often act as a signal that too many investors have moved in one direction, which can render a correction increasingly likely. \par \par \uc2  The dollar showed some signs of life in overnight trading after Friday's slide to a one-month low against the euro of $1.1327. However, it gave back most of its gains after a report showing a 0.1% rise in August industrial production, below economists' expectations of a 0.3% increase. \par \par \uc2  Much of the attention in the market now will turn to the meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven next weekend in Dubai, particularly with the growing pressure on China to revalue its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or at least introduce some flexibility to its \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. \par \par \uc2  Ahead of the G7 -- of which China isn't a member -- a key Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  committee Monday said a wider trading band is the most likely next step in the country's gradual shift to a more flexible exchange-rate system. His comments reinforced a belief that China won't make any dramatic changes to its yuan policy. \par \par \uc2  Also, Standard & Poor's said a free float of the yuan could damage China's sovereign credit rating and the creditworthiness of its local banks. \par \par \uc2  Though Beijing says the yuan exchange rate is determined through a managed float, in reality the rate is almost entirely government-controlled, and it gyrates in a narrow band with a central point of around 8.28 yuan against the dollar. As a result, the yuan has weakened against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in line with the dollar, which critics say gives it an unfair competitive edge on global markets. \par \par \uc2  -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385; tom.barkley@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (Jamie McGeever contributed to this story.) \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  09-15-03 1713ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030915dz9f004h6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
