\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Valuations In Focus \b0\par\par\uc2 1,337 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2003\par 07:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: Bargain Hunters Boost Shares Despite Data\par DJIA          9471.55    gain 11.79     up 0.1%\par NASDAQ        1855.03    gain 8.94      up 0.5%\par S&P 500       1018.63    gain 2.21      up 0.2%\par Dow Future    9476.00    gain 3.00         0.0%\par NASDAQ Future 1358.00    loss 1.50      dn 0.1%\par S&P Future    1019.25    gain 1.25      up 0.1%\par 10-Yr US Treasury: 4.25% down 0.06\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)-  A burst of late-day buying lifted Wall Street modestly higher Friday despite a lackluster retail sales report and disappointing sales of new software licenses by Oracle Corp. Prices of Treasurys gained. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS: Analysts said investors decided to take advantage of some bargains after the Dow Jones industrials lost as much as 78.81 earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  "Generally speaking, people are optimistic, but we're now at valuations that are appropriate as long as the economic and profit picture continue to improve as they have been," said Susan L. Malley, chief investment officer for Malley Associates Capital Management in New York. \par \par \uc2  "As we move into the earnings preannouncement season, people are standing pat in case there are any bad surprises," she said. \par \par \uc2  The fledgling competition to develop "smarter" cell phones for Web-hungry consumers is getting a powerful new tag-team entry: Motorola Inc. and Microsoft Corp. The U.S.'s largest cell-phone and software manufacturers plan to announce an alliance Monday under which Microsoft software will power. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, WTO talks collapsed in a severe blow to the future of global trade talks. Negotiations had hinged on how deeply rich nations would cut their farm subsidies. But the breakdown came over investment and antitrust policies. \par \par \uc2  BONDS: Treasurys prices rose Friday after reports showed unexpectedly soft retail sales levels and diminished consumer confidence, but those gains faded a bit into the close of the session. \par \par \uc2  The government said retail sales rose 0.6% last month, which would be a hopeful number for growth in most cases. But a Dow Jones-CNBC survey of economists had predicted that overall retail sales would rise by 1.6%. \par \par \uc2  "While this report is weaker than expectations, it is not a weak retail sales report," Bear Stearns chief U.S. economist John Ryding told clients in a note. Given the performance of the report's various measures, "there is nothing here to lead us to believe that we should be lowering our GDP growth estimate of 5% for the third quarter," he said. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the University of Michigan's mid-month report on consumer sentiment for September decreased to 88.2 from 89.3 in August and 90.9 in July. Economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires had expected the index, which is based on a telephone survey of around 300 people, to have risen to 90.0 in mid-September. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Mkts Little Changed With Japan Closed\par USD-Yen             117.32       gain  0.01    up 0.01%\par AUD-USD             0.6624       loss  0.0007  dn 0.1%\par Nikkei 225          Closed\par Hang Seng           10955.44     gain  71.92   up 0.7%\par JGB Yield           Closed\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par HONG KONG (Dow Jones)--Stock and bond markets in the region were\par little changed in light activity Monday with Japan closed for a holiday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par China doesn't plan to revalue its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the near term, despite\par speculative and political pressure to do so, state media said Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par China will keep the value of its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the yuan, stable but\par gradually allow it to trade in a wider range, the state-run Economic\par Daily quoted Li Yang, a member of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's monetary policy\par committee, as saying.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yuan has traded in a narrow range around 8.28 yuan per U.S. dollar\par since 1994.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Beijing has promised to gradually loosen controls. It has balked,\par however, at pressure from the United States and other trading partners\par to let the yuan trade freely, saying it needs time to get its banks and\par financial markets into better shape.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Markets Set For Directionless Open\par Euro-USD            1.1278      loss  0.0015  dn 0.1%\par Stlg-USD            1.6041      gain  0.0011  up 0.1%\par USD-Franc           1.3815      gain  0.0014  up 0.1%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares and prices of government bonds\par should start the session little changed, although some issues could show\par slight gains. The euro starts the week in a range.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: European shares are expected to open little changed to\par slightly higher, picking up some of Friday's gains on Wall Street but\par then stalling on the lack of any further significant impulse.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par European stocks were lower Friday as investors took profits after the\par key U.S. economic reports undermined confidence in the pace of global\par profit recovery.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par David Page, economist at Investec, said the data prompted the question\par whether market gains have outpaced the economic reality. "The market has\par priced in a much more aggressive recovery and may now have to readjust\par expectations," he said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. oil companies ChevronTexaco Corp. and Exxon Mobil Corp. are\par expected to submit rival preliminary proposals as early as this week to\par buy a 25% interest in big Russian oil producer Yukos-Sibneft, say people\par familiar with the situation. That size stake is valued at about $11\par billion based on current market prices.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: Euro-zone government bond prices may start little changed after\par Friday's rally on the weaker-than-expected U.S. data, a strong euro and\par lower stock markets.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Yields dropped across the euro-zone curve by 10 to 14 basis points,\par with the front end and five-year maturities outperforming the longer\par end.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro starts the session at around $1.1275 after trading in\par a range overnight of around $1.1255 to $1.1285.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The G7 meeting in Dubai looms large over the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par this week, with the sharpening focus on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and China's tightly controlled exchange rate likely to lead\par to nervous, volatile trading.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A U.S. interest rate decision Tuesday from the Federal Reserve - and\par the closely watched accompanying statement - as well as U.S. economic\par indicators throughout the week will also influence the dollar's moves.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And Sweden's decision Sunday to stay out of the euro will be a factor\par driving the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Sweden's government won't stage a second\par referendum on whether to adopt the euro before 2010, Prime Minister\par Goeran Persson said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Monday, Sep 15: UK Prices, U.S. Capacity Use\par ET/GMT\par 0300/0700 Italy        Istat final August Consumer Price Index\par 0430/0830 U.K.         Consumer Prices\par 0500/0900 Italy        Treasury auctions BTPs and CCTs\par 0830/1230 U.S.         July Business Inventories (last +0.1%)\par 0830/1230 U.S.         Second-Quarter Current Account Deficit\par 0830/1230 U.S.         September Empire State Manufacturing\par 0915/1315 U.S.         August Capacity Utilization\par 0915/1315 U.S.         August Industrial Production\par 0930/1330 E.U.         European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  calls for bids\par    N/A    Germany      Bundesbank allocates EUR6B on auction\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181;\par dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Thales (F.THL): 1H Earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 5 analysts) EUR32M (EUR88M)\par Note: Operating profit seen down 6.4% at EUR278 million vs EUR297\par million. 1H revenue data already released: down 8.5% to EUR4.56\par billion.\par Zodiac (F.ZOD): Preliminary FY Revenue\par Consensus net profit before items (JCF, 8 analysts): EUR166.6M (N/A)\par Forecast range: EUR138.1M - EUR184.5M\par Consensus sales: EUR1.53B (N/A)\par Bricorama (F.BMA): 1H Earnings\par JC Decaux (F.JCD): 1H Earnings\par Coca-Cola HBC (I.COC): EGM\par Banco De Valencia (E.BVA): Last Day Of 1X15 Share Issue\par Prosegur (E.PRO): To Pay EUR0.119 Dividend\par Biofocus (U.BFS): 1H Earnings\par Enterprise (U.EPC): 1H Earnings\par Just Car Clinics (U.JCR): 1H Earnings\par Parkwood Holdings (U.PWH): 1H Earnings\par Tarsus (U.TRS): 1H Earnings\par Wilmington (U.WTN): 1H Earnings\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 15-09-03 0501GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1011 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030915dz9f0009d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picsc