\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Speculators Net Long Yen Positions At 4-Yr High \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Barkley \par \uc2 586 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 September 2003\par 18:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators upped the ante in the week ending Sept. 9 on their bet that the yen would continue to rally, but analysts believe many of them withdrew towards the end of last week after seeing Japan's apparent resolve in keeping its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from strengthening too much. \par \par \uc2  The latest positioning data from the International Monetary Market on the Chicago \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures exchange show traders held a massive net long yen positions of 52,845 contracts in the week to Sept. 9, up from 46,955 the week before. \par \par \uc2  That marks a new four-year high and the second-largest net long yen position on record. Extreme positioning can often act as a signal that too many investors have moved in one direction, which can render a correction increasingly likely. \par \par \uc2  At first glance, the data suggest the Japanese government has a formidable task on its hands in seeking to tame the rampaging yen and limit the damage to a nascent, export-led economic recovery. The IMM report also raises questions about the extent to which \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - under which it mostly buys dollars for yen - is having the classic effect of flushing speculators, those who take a shorter-term view of trading, out of the markets. \par \par \uc2  But analysts think this may have worked better towards the end of the week and that the net long position was probably reduced following ongoing reported bouts of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese government, via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It seems like the speculative community definitely got overextended, and I think they've been pared back, which allows some of the pressure (for a stronger yen) to be taken off," said Lauren Germain, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Banc of American Securities in New York. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, ABN-Amro noted in a report that another indicator, that captures interbank market data, showed a smaller net-long yen position among speculators. \par \par \uc2  Acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the BOJ is believed by some analysts to have pumped as much as $10 billion into the market last week to prevent the yen from dipping towards the Y115 level many consider to be the pain threshold that could derail Japan's export-led recovery. \par \par \uc2  Speculators also appeared to be betting that Japanese authorities wouldn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  during U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow's visit to Japan and Asia early this month. The latest data capture market positions established during Snow's trip to Asia or immediately following it. \par \par \uc2  "They were testing the MOF's resolve to really protect the exchange rate, which they have done very strictly," said Germain, who expects further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The BOJ was suspected to have entered the market almost every day last week, stopping the dollar from breaking below Y116 early in the week, and by Friday it was apparently protecting support at Y117. \par \par \uc2  Around midday Monday in New York, the dollar was at Y117.47, up from Y117.33 late Friday in New York. \par \par \uc2  Germain expects the dollar to move slightly higher, but remain rangebound due to "pressure on Asia as a region to appreciate" on the back of an improved economic outlook. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, speculators increased their net long euro position against the dollar to 2,505, but not enough to indicate a potential change in market direction. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385\par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030915dz9f006sh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
