\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar pummeled as economic recovery in limbo.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez  \par \uc2 675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2003\par 22:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 12 (Reuters) - The dollar tumbled against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, as investors grew alarmed by a plethora of negative economic reports that seemed to quash expectations of rapid growth in the United States.  \par \par \uc2 But the greenback defied its overall weak trend as it rose against the yen amid market suspicions the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was undertaking yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'  \par \par \uc2 The euro climbed to $1.1284 against the dollar, up 0.70 percent from Thursday's New York close. Against the yen, the dollar rose 0.2 percent to 117.28 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was likewise firmer against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , up 0.98 percent at 132.39 yen.  \par \par \uc2 But the greenback fell 0.26 percent vs the Swiss franc to 1.3793 francs. Sterling also notched fairly steep gains against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , up 0.51 percent at $1.6026.  \par \par \uc2 Some analysts questioned the sustainability of growth in the United States given a still anemic jobs situation, although they are not ruling out the possibility the U.S. economy may bounce back in the next few months.  \par \par \uc2 "It's just a matter of time before jobs growth catches up with the whole U.S. recovery," said Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of New York. "We are not changing our forecasts on U.S. growth even with the negative data," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Expectations about a more vigorous economic rebound were again dampened on Friday as U.S. consumer confidence and retail sales data came out weaker than expected. But some analysts said the underlying figures were not as grim as the headline numbers showed.  \par \par \uc2 The University of Michigan's preliminary consumer sentiment survey for September fell to 88.2, below expectations for a rise to 90.0 from August's reading of 89.3.  \par \par \uc2 Consumer sentiment figures followed U.S. retail sales and inflation data which on first blush appeared worse than expected, although the reports showed underlying trends remained strong.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is taking its cue from the headlines and were looking for blockbuster numbers, and although they didn't get them, the underlying trends are still firm," said Marcel Kasumovich, head of G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Merrill Lynch in New York.  \par \par \uc2 But given the spate of negative data, analysts said the market's focus has reverted back to the U.S. economy's structural weaknesses, primarily the current account - a broad measure of global trade - and budget deficits.  \par \par \uc2 "Structural headwinds such as the twin deficits are starting to come back into focus," said Tim Stewart, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Morgan Stanley in New York.  \par \par \uc2 SWEDISH REFERENDUM ON EURO  \par \par \uc2 Markets are also closely watching the outcome of a referendum over the weekend in Sweden concerning its membership in the European economic and monetary union. Sweden's Finance Minister Bosse Ringholm said on Friday he was still hopeful his country would vote to join the euro but added there would be no new vote for a decade if euro membership was rejected.  \par \par \uc2 Ahead of the vote, the euro was trading up around 0.2 percent against the crown.  \par \par \uc2 BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  IN DOLLAR, EURO?  \par \par \uc2 In contrast to its generalized descent across the board, the dollar firmed against the yen on Friday on suspicions the Bank of Japan was once again in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets undertaking yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders cited Japanese banks aggressively buying dollars and selling yen.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is certainly of the impression that the BoJ is in the market in both the dollar/yen and euro/yen trades," said Cyrus Whitney, head dealer at Commerzbank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 A Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MoF) official in New York declined to comment when asked if the BoJ was in the market on Friday, selling the yen against the dollar and euro.  \par \par \uc2 The BoJ, which acts on behalf of the MoF, periodically \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by selling yen and buying dollars to protect its exporters' competitive advantage. (Additional reporting by Daniel Bases).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030912dz9c006fn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
