\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar sell-off deepens after weak consumer data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2003\par 16:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 12 (Reuters) - The dollar fell on Friday after economic reports left a mixed view on the U.S. economy, but notably consumer sentiment weakened in the early part of September, casting a negative light on the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 However, traders cited Japanese banks aggressively buying dollars and selling yen, leaving the dollar higher against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and at odds with the general trend in the market of dollar weakness.  \par \par \uc2 The University of Michigan's preliminary consumer sentiment survey for September fell to 88.2, below expectations for a rise to 90.0 from August's final reading of 89.3.  \par \par \uc2 "It does not bode well for the U.S. economy considering the recent weakness in the labor market. It is negative for the dollar, which translates into euro buying," said Patrick Brodie, senior trader at SMBC in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Early dollar weakness ensued after U.S. retail sales and inflation data on first blush appeared worse than expected, although analysts said the reports also showed underlying trends remained strong.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market is taking its cue from the headlines and were looking for blockbuster numbers, and although they didn't get them, the underlying trends are still firm," said Marcel Kasumovich, head of G-10 \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Merrill Lynch in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Headline August U.S. retail sales rose 0.6 percent, below expectations, however excluding the automobile sector, sales rose 0.7 percent, close to the Reuters forecast.  \par \par \uc2 "This will be viewed as a slight negative for the dollar because of the weakness in the headline number. But any drop will be limited by the solid gain in sales outside the auto sector," said Paul Ferley, assistant chief economist at BMO Financial Group in Toronto.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. producer prices rose 0.4 percent in August, more than expected, but excluding the volatile food and energy sectors, prices only rose 0.1 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The euro surged to a fresh four-week high of $1.1324 after the consumer sentiment data before slipping back to $1.1297, still a gain of 0.80 percent Thursday's New York close after having spent much of the session below the $1.1190 level.  \par \par \uc2 The euro rose to 132.59 yen, up 1.13 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar traded at 117.31 yen, up 0.23 percent on the day, but down from the session high 117.52 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said Japanese banks were aggressive buyers of dollars for yen in mid-morning New York trade.  \par \par \uc2 "It is the usual suspects buying dollar/yen very aggressively above 117.00 yen to 117.50 where investment banks came into sell," said Brodie.  \par \par \uc2 A Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official in New York declined to comment when asked by Reuters if the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on Friday in order to weaken the yen, against the dollar and the euro.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030912dz9c004mz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
