\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Kuroda cautiously optimistic on Japan economy.  \b0\par\par\uc2 449 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2003\par 16:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Sept 12 (Reuters) - Haruhiko Kuroda, formerly Japan's top financial diplomat and now a special adviser to the Cabinet, expressed guarded confidence about Japan's economic recovery on Friday.   \par \par \uc2  "I am cautiously optimistic about the Japanese economy," with optimism tempered "because of persistent deflation and structural reforms needed for the banking sector," Kuroda said in his prepared remarks.   \par \par \uc2  Even as the world's second biggest economy appears to be gaining momentum after a long period of stagnation, the country's banks remain saddled with a mountain of non-performing loans and bad debts.   \par \par \uc2  Kuroda was delivering an address entitled "Is Japan's economic recovery sustainable?" to an audience at the Council on Foreign Relations in New York.   \par \par \uc2  On the subject of yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders believe \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has undertaken in recent days, Kuroda said, "the international community must accept a weaker yen."   \par \par \uc2  Asked by reporters whether the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has engaged in yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  this month, Kuroda declined comment. Some commercial banks estimate the BoJ may have bought around $15 billion of dollars over about the past week.   \par \par \uc2  Replying to reporters questions Kuroda added that, "The Japanese government has no target at the moment when it comes to the dollar/yen rate."   \par \par \uc2  But he added that "the yen is overvalued according to purchasing power parity", which is a widely used method for calculating a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fair value.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders believe that Japanese authorities have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen during recent sessions, displeased with the dollar's fall to near four-month lows last week below 116 yen. On Friday morning in New York, the dollar was trading at 117.33 yen  up 0.3 percent on the session.   \par \par \uc2  In the event of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  acts on behalf of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  Kuroda also spoke about the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  "The Chinese renminbi is grossly undervalued according to purchasing power parity principle," Kuroda told reporters.   \par \par \uc2  Earlier this month, Kuroda said Beijing should phase in a revaluation of the yuan to ensure a smooth transition.   \par \par \uc2  Kuroda then said China should not wait too long to review its policy on the yuan, which is pegged in a narrow range around 8.28 to the dollar.   \par \par \uc2  Japan's government is concerned to check the appreciation of the yen in order to protect the competitiveness of Japanese exporters and nurture a rebound in the domestic economy.   \par \par \uc2  Kuroda said in his remarks that "for Japan's economic growth to be sustainable, we need a rate of inflation of 2 percent," which Japan's economy does not currently have, he said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030912dz9c003xx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
