\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Markets Search For Direction \b0\par\par\uc2 1,815 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 September 2003\par 06:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: Patriotic Rally On Sept. 11\par DJIA          9459.76    gain 39.30     up 0.4%\par NASDAQ        1846.09    gain 22.28     up 1.2%\par S&P 500       1016.42    gain 5.50      up 0.5%\par Dow Future    9461.00    gain 1.00         0.0%\par NASDAQ Future 1352.00    loss 0.50         0.0%\par S&P Future    1016.0     loss 0.75      dn 0.1%\par 10-Yr US Treasury: 4.31% up 0.02\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Stocks rose for the first time in three sessions Thursday while Treasurys shaved recent price gains. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS: The market's course was choppy as investors weighed upbeat earnings from Adobe Systems Inc. against a brokerage downgrade of IBM Corp. Trading was light and Wall Street subdued on the second anniversary of the Sept. 11 terror attacks. \par \par \uc2  "Today, we have a little bit of a patriotic rally," said Michael Murphy, head trader at Wachovia Securities in Baltimore, of Thursday's comeback. \par \par \uc2  With positive economic news more prevalent than negative data, the market is able to more easily shrug off disappointments. On Thursday it managed to advance despite discouraging news from the Labor Department, which said new claims for unemployment insurance filed last week increased by a seasonally adjusted 3,000 to 422,000, a two-month high. \par \par \uc2  Given that the economy still has its soft spots, some are concerned that stocks have climbed too much. \par \par \uc2  "The market is overextended but it just doesn't want to come down," Murphy said. "There is still more of a risk to be out of the market than in, but I would like to see it correct a little bit." \par \par \uc2  On Thursday Merrill Lynch & Co. Inc. raised its rating on Adobe to "buy" from "neutral," while JMP Securities upgraded it to "market outperform" from "market perform." \par \par \uc2  IBM advanced 8 cents to $87.92 after losing as much as $1.44 earlier, when Smith Barney downgraded it to "underperform" from "in-line," saying the stock is overvalued. \par \par \uc2  BONDS: The addition of $13 billion of 10-year notes helped keep Treasurys under water Thursday in subdued trade. \par \par \uc2  Traders and investors reported only light activity with the auction of the reopened 10-year Treasury benchmarks the only event of note. Even an unexpected rise in joblessness sparked only a short-lived flurry of buying that quickly fizzled out. \par \par \uc2  Treasury awarded the reopened 10-year notes at a yield of 4.34%, slightly cheaper than market participants had expected. Appetite for the paper was strong with the bid-to-cover ratio, a measure of demand, reported at 2.23, well above the 1.95 average of the last 10 auctions of this maturity. \par \par \uc2  The jobless figures gave the market pause. \par \par \uc2  "The second consecutive claims figure substantially above 400,000 last week is particularly discouraging," said Jade Zelnik, chief economist at RBS Greenwich Capital in Greenwich Conn. "The disappointing pickup in the pace of layoffs reinforces the likelihood that the Federal Open Market Committee will continue to emphasize the large amount of slack in the economy and downside risks with respect to inflation." \par \par \uc2  In other data, the U.S. trade deficit widened moderately to $40.32 billion in July from a revised $40.04 billion the month before as purchases of foreign goods and services climbed to their second highest level on record. \par \par \uc2  Drew Matus, economist at Lehman Brothers in New York, said "the data tells us that the story for the economy remains the same." While the economy is likely to grow by "at least 5%" in the third quarter, "the labor market is likely to remain weak." \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Tokyo Stocks Manage Modest Comeback\par USD-Yen             117.05       loss  0.01    dn 0.01%\par AUD-USD             0.6595       loss  0.0007  dn 0.1%\par Nikkei 225          10659.96     gain  113.63  up 1.1%\par Hang Seng           Closed\par JGB Yield           1.4800%      unchanged\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo's main stock index advanced Friday morning,\par with blue chip technology and bank shares getting a boost from Wall\par Street's tech-led surge on the anniversary of the Sept. 11 attacks.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the buying mood in Tokyo was expected to peter out toward the end\par of the session as investors trim their holdings ahead of a three-day\par holiday weekend, traders said. Japanese financial markets will close on\par Monday for a national holiday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the dollar was slightly lower against the yen, a day\par after Japanese finance authorities reportedly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market\par to support the dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Zembei Mizoguchi, the Finance Ministry's point-man on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , indicated early Friday that authorities were keeping a\par close watch on the yen's strength and might act again.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par North Korea has once again rejected a U.S. demand that it give up its\par nuclear weapons program, saying it has no intention of disarming itself\par completely.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow said his upcoming trip to the\par Middle East and Asia would represent a renewed push to choke off\par terrorist financing.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Markets To Drift\par Euro-USD            1.1188      loss  0.0018  dn 0.2%\par Stlg-USD            1.5959      gain  0.0012  up 0.08%\par USD-Franc           1.3866      gain  0.0025  up 0.2%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares, government bonds and the euro are\par expected to open little changed as the markets seek near-term\par directions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: European shares are expected to open mixed or slightly higher\par and then level off ahead of the U.S. retail sales figures. Some\par investors may opt for profit-taking going into the weekend.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par London spreadbettor City Index is calling the FTSE up 10 to 4252 and\par the DAX down 10 to 3556. European stocks were mostly higher Thursday, as\par auto stocks rebounded after several sessions in the red.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Strategists at Investec said positive earnings momentum should drive\par shares higher in the next few months. "We do remain conscious, however,\par that without continuing favorable newsflow, many cyclical sectors are\par looking fully valued and momentum could easily stall if the\par macroeconomic picture deteriorates," they said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the news, the E.U. moved closer to easing rules on discount drug\par sales, serving a blow to the pharmaceutical industry, as a top adviser\par to the European Court of Justice said German practices blocking some\par cut-price imports are illegal.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: Euro-zone government bond are expected to open little changed\par to slightly lower ahead of the U.S. data.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro-zone curve flattened slightly Thursday, despite the fact that\par supply is coming in at the long end.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro-zone finance ministers are expected to talk up the chances of\par recovery Friday even as the bloc's biggest economies slump into\par recession and their governments slip deeper into debt.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Host nation Italy became the latest euro-zone member to officially\par enter into recession this week, with the news that its economy\par contracted by 0.1% for the second successive quarter.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Among U.S. figures, investors will focus on retail sales and consumer\par confidence.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro drifted lower overnight, starting the European session\par at around $1.1185.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Initially selling off sharply on yet another gloomy assessment of the\par U.S. labor market Thursday, the dollar then rebounded strongly on the\par euro's failure to move substantially higher.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On top of this, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's monetary authorities were said by traders to\par have \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market once again, this time to push the dollar\par back up through Y117.00 when heavy selling from European banks had sent\par it hurtling as low as Y116.47.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Now we're back to square one," said one U.S.-based trader. "It's like\par that crazy drop (in dollar/yen) never happened."\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The euro also ended in New York marginally weaker against the yen, at\par Y131.17 compared with Y131.32 late Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow said he will repeat his view at an\par upcoming meeting of Group of Eight finance ministers that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should\par keep its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to a minimum.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Swedish Prime Minister Goeran Persson said a referendum on whether the\par nation should join the euro will proceed this weekend despite the death\par of the foreign minister.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Friday, Sep 12: France Production, US Retail\par ET/GMT\par 0245/0645 France       July Trade Balance\par 0250/0650 France       July Industrial Production\par 0830/1230 U.S.         August PPI (last +0.1%)\par 0830/1230 U.S.         August Retail and Food Sales\par 0840/1240 E.U.         European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  member Issing\par 1000/1400 Italy        Euro-zone finance ministers meet\par 1000/1400 U.S.         Second-Quarter Manufacturers' Profits\par 1000/1400 U.S.         University of Michigan September\par                        Consumer Sentiment (last 89.3)\par    N/A    Japan        Bank Of Japan Policy Board meeting\par    N/A    Germany      Bundesbank calls for bids on EUR6B\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181;\par dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par RAS (I.RAS) 1H Earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 5 analysts): EUR288.2M (EUR937M)\par Consensus sales: EUR9.28B (EUR8.22B)\par Note: Eyes and ears on conference call for on any indication about what\par new 3-yr plan, to be presented in Oct, will focus on.\par Finmeccanica (I.MEC): 1H Earnings\par Consensus EBITA (DJ, 3 analysts): EUR180M (EUR172M)\par Consensus sales: EUR4B (EUR6.54B)\par Note: Market looking out for further details, especially to margins and\par spending prospects, of recent JV with BAE Systems.\par Sanpaolo IMI (IMI): 1H Earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 27 analysts): EUR284M (EUR324M)\par Consensus sales: EUR1.9B (EUR1.9B)\par Note: Analysts expect there to have been pressure on margins due to\par integration costs, low top-line growth. After 44% rise in the shares\par this year, some analysts see profit taking on heels of the results.\par But they expect bank to benefit most from equity, macro pick-up.\par Intercontinental Hotels (IHG): 1H Earnings\par Consensus pretax profit before exceptionals items (DJ, 3 analysts):\par GBP72M (GBP108M)\par Note:Analysts expect the market to focus on the outlook statement, in\par addition to any update on the cost-cutting program and additional\par information on the progress of asset disposals.\par Kudelski (Z.KUD): 1H Earnings\par Consensus net loss (DJ, 7 analysts): CHF10.5M (CHF17.9M)\par Consensus sales: CHF167.6M (CHF165.7M)\par Note: Analysts want news on integration of recently acquired Mediaguard\par and JV with Thomson.\par LVMH (F.LVH): 1H Earnings\par Note: Analysts expect LVMH to report 1H profit before goodwill\par amortization +23% to EUR429 million from EUR350 million last time,\par according to survey of 8 forecasts. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  hedging policy, cost\par controls strength of core Vuitton brand seen offsetting weakness of\par luxury market. Little doubt on operating profit after company's July\par guidance that 1H saw growth of 3% from last year's EUR840 million:\par that would mean 1H 03 operating earnings of EUR865 million.\par Fraport (F.FRP): August Traffic Figures\par Heidelberger Druck (G.HBD): AGM\par Eliza Tinsley (U.TNL): AGM\par NXT (U.NTX): FY Earnings\par Photo-Scan (U.PTO): 1H Earnings\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 12-09-03 0459GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1011 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030912dz9c0006r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
