\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar inches up vs yen as  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  looms.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano  \par \uc2 641 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2003\par 08:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 11 (Reuters) - The dollar edged up against the yen on Thursday on wariness about Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , unfazed by worries surrounding the second anniversary of the September 11 attacks.  \par \par \uc2 Still, lingering concerns about the outlook for the U.S. economy following last week's weak jobs data kept most market players from bidding up the dollar sharply, resulting in range-bound trade.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback was hovering around 117.19/21 yen at 0555 GMT, up slightly from late U.S. levels around 117.10 yen, with traders suspecting that persistent bids seen around 117 yen were from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese authorities are said to have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  intermittently since the start of the week as they were displeased with the dollar's fall last week below 116 yen - its lowest level in nearly four months.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers say Tokyo is determined to defend the 115 yen level, a key chart point, out of concern that a higher yen would derail Japan's export-reliant economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 A sharp fall in the Tokyo stock market's Nikkei average helped to curb the yen's recent buoyancy, although fresh data suggested that foreign investors' Japanese stock buying remained brisk.  \par \par \uc2 Weekly capital flows data from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed that foreign investors were net buyers of Japanese equities last week for the 21st consecutive week, with their purchases rising to 488.3 billion yen from 326.1 billion yen a week earlier.  \par \par \uc2 On the other hand, doubts about the sustainability of the current U.S. recovery weighed on the dollar, dominated by worries that the recovery may have been lifted mainly by tax cuts and the government's military spending.  \par \par \uc2 "It will be impossible for the U.S. to keep expanding the budget deficit. It's obvious U.S. fiscal conditions will be a disturbing factor after 2005 (when tax cuts are expected to be scaled back)," said Seiya Nakajima, chief economist at Itochu Corp.  \par \par \uc2 "To be sure, demand is growing in the U.S. at present. But I'm not sure if company managers are ready to boost fixed spending, especially employment, without a conviction that the economy will be stable in the next five to 10-years," he said.  \par \par \uc2 Amid concerns about U.S. employment, the market will likely keep a close eye on figures for U.S. weekly jobless claims due out at 1230 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 ANNIVERSARY OF ATTACKS  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was little rattled by the emergence of what was said to be new footage of Osama bin Laden and audio of the al Qaeda leader praising the September 11 attacks against the United States, although Wall Street stock prices dived on Tuesday on the news.  \par \par \uc2 Dollar bulls hope the greenback will be bought once September 11 passes without serious incident, especially given the high number of long-yen/dollar-short positions among speculative accounts, shown in the weekly commitment of traders report from the U.S. Commodity and Futures Trade Commission.  \par \par \uc2 "I expect short-covering to push the dollar to above 117.50 in the near term," said Shogo Nagaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Trust and Banking.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was a tad higher versus the dollar at $1.1218/24 compared with around $1.12 in late New York, and edged up to 131.44/51 yen from 131.25 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Still, the euro was overshadowed by the probability that Sweden will vote "no" to taking on the euro in a referendum on Sunday.  \par \par \uc2 Although some traders think the Swedish rejection of the euro has already been priced in, the market is still wary of the vote hurting confidence in the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Elsewhere, the Australian dollar shot up about 0.5 percent to 65.89/94 U.S. cents after surprisingly strong Australian job data.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030911dz9b001uy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
