\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar steady as Sept 11 anxieties taken in stride.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima  \par \uc2 491 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2003\par 03:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 11 (Reuters) - The dollar was steady to firmer on Thursday, unfazed by worries surrounding the second anniversary of the September 11 attacks, with persistent wariness of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  adding support against the yen.   \par \par \uc2  Although Wall Street stocks sank on Wednesday after al Jazeera Arabic television released a new tape which it said was audio of al Qaeda leader Osama bin Laden praising the attacks against the United States, the dollar maintained its poise.   \par \par \uc2  "Looking at dollar/yen and euro/dollar, it's difficult to tell how much of the dollar's gains was due to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and concerns over the European economy," said Hiroyuki Watanabe, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Shinsei Bank.   \par \par \uc2  "But if you also look at the dollar's moves against all the other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the same time, you can say the consensus in the market is that the bin Laden tape wasn't such a big factor."   \par \par \uc2  The dollar hovered at around 117.10/15 yen at 0045 GMT, little changed after rising late on Wednesday on talk that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during New York hours.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar stood at around $1.1185 to the euro gaining about a quarter cent from Wednesday's late New York levels.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WARINESS   \par \par \uc2  The Japanese authorities are said to have been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  intermittently since the start of the week, displeased with the dollar's fall last week below 116 yen, its lowest level in nearly four months.   \par \par \uc2  Dealers say Tokyo is determined to defend the 115 yen level, a key chart point, out of concern that a higher yen would derail Japan's export-reliant economic recovery.   \par \par \uc2  A sharp fall in the Tokyo stock market's Nikkei average helped to curb the yen's recent buoyancy, although fresh data suggested that foreign investors' Japanese stock buying remained brisk.   \par \par \uc2  Weekly capital flows data from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed that foreign investors were net buyers of Japanese equities last week for the 21st consecutive week, with their purchases rising to 488.3 billion yen from 326.1 billion yen a week earlier.   \par \par \uc2  Watanabe at Shinsei Bank said the high level of long yen positions among speculative accounts, shown in the weekly commitment of traders report from the U.S. Commodity and Futures Trade Commission, suggests that the dollar may soon benefit from unwinding of such long yen positions.   \par \par \uc2  "If nothing happens on September 11, those who had put on long yen positions to hedge against any dollar-bearish incidents could liquidate them," he said. Traders said the Bank of Japan's two-day policy meeting starting later on Thursday was unlikely to have much impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as no policy change was expected.   \par \par \uc2  The market will likely keep a close eye on U.S. weekly jobless claims figures due out at 1230 GMT, given weaker-than-expected non-farm payrolls figures last Friday.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030911dz9b00022\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
