\par\par \b \uc2  Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Edges Up In Quiet Mkt On 9/11 Anniversary \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jonathan Soble \par \uc2 801 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2003\par 07:44\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- The dollar edged higher against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia Thursday but subdued trading on the second anniversary of the Sept. 11 terror attacks kept the major pairs in tight ranges. \par \par \uc2  The anniversary of the attacks on New York and Washington, coupled with the release Wednesday of a videotape apparently containing new footage of Osama bin Laden, put fresh focus on the global political uncertainties faced by the U.S., traders said. \par \par \uc2  "People are a bit nervous that something could happen today," said the head trader at a foreign brokerage in Tokyo. "But it's not just the threat today - it's the threat tomorrow, it's Iraq, it's a lot of things." \par \par \uc2  The date aside, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has also been tamed by vigorous \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities since late last week. \par \par \uc2  Although market sentiment remained roundly bearish on the U.S. unit against the yen, with the Japanese authorities believed to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  daily since last Thursday to stem the dollar's fall, few traders remained willing to test the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair's downside. \par \par \uc2  "There's a feeling that it won't fall no matter how much you sell, so why bother?" said Satoshi Tate, vice president for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at UFJ Bank. "People are tired." \par \par \uc2  Japan's Finance Ministry doesn't reveal when it's been in the market or by how much it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted Thursday by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drain its current account surplus suggested Tokyo sold at least Y300 billion Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducts \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the ministry. \par \par \uc2  That amount - some traders pegged the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at closer to Y500 billion - comes on top of an estimated Y1.2 trillion spent since the beginning of the month to keep the yen from strengthening. \par \par \uc2  But while the MOF has succeeded in preventing a dollar collapse for now, some traders and analysts questioned how long it can keep up its single-handed defense of the greenback. \par \par \uc2  "The only buying interest is from the authorities," said the foreign brokerage dealer. "That's not sustainable." \par \par \uc2  With Japan on Wednesday raising its estimate of second-quarter economic growth to an annualized rate of 3.9% from the preliminary 2.3% - the fastest growth among Group of Seven industrialized countries - Tokyo could find it harder to justify export-boosting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , J.P. Morgan analysts said in a research note. \par \par \uc2  Morgan cut its forecast for the dollar to Y115 at the end of September from Y117; Y110 at the end of December from Y115; and Y105 at the end of March from Y110. \par \par \uc2  "Any overt criticism (from the U.S. of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) would be greeted with further downward pressure," the analysts said. \par \par \uc2  At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y117.17, slightly above Y117.07 late Wednesday in New York. The dollar was at Y116.85 late Wednesday in Tokyo. \par \par \uc2  The euro was at $1.1205, lower than $1.1218 late Wednesday in New York. The euro was at $1.1223 in Tokyo Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y131.32, up from Y131.28 in New York late Wednesday. The euro was at Y131.14 in Tokyo late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Sterling was at $1.5907, up a hair from $1.5903 in New York but lower than $1.5913 in Tokyo Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  A rough day for Japanese equities also helped keep the yen in check, with the benchmark Nikkei 225 Stock Average shedding 2.9% of its value. \par \par \uc2  "There's no incentive to buy the yen at all today," UFJ's Tate said. \par \par \uc2  With neither the dollar nor the yen showing much promise, short-term speculators took advantage of an early dip in the euro Wednesday to buy the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said. \par \par \uc2  In a late rally led by foreign players, the euro hit fresh three-week highs of $1.1245 and Y131.81. \par \par \uc2  "Basically, people are bearish on the dollar, but the yen is protected (from rising) by the MOF, so that leaves the euro," said the foreign brokerage dealer. \par \par \uc2  The euro is set for a test of $1.1350, the 50% retracement of its fall from its late-May peak, in the short term, he said. \par \par \uc2  -By Jonathan Soble, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; jonathan.soble@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by Alastair Gale \par \par \uc2  The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly. \par \par \uc2  Subscribers can find this table: \par \par \uc2  -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087. \par \par \uc2  -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX \par \par \uc2  -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT \par \par \uc2  -On Bloomberg b