\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar climbs haltingly amid economic concerns.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \uc2 652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2003\par 19:43\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 10 (Reuters) - The dollar drifted higher on Wednesday but without much conviction as doubts lingered over the pace of the U.S. economic recovery, leaving the greenback near three-week lows versus the euro and sterling.  \par \par \uc2 Persistent concerns in the market that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the yen from gaining against the dollar left the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  steady to higher on the day.  \par \par \uc2 Doubts about the U.S. economic recovery were highlighted last week by a surprisingly weak U.S. jobs report that dove-tailed with talk from Federal Reserve officials that U.S. benchmark interest rates are likely to remain at 1 percent, a 45-year low.  \par \par \uc2 "Investors are still absorbing the idea that the Fed will stay on hold a bit longer than had been expected," said David Durrant, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank Julius Baer in New York. "There is a tiny bit of a search for yield," driving interest away from the dollar, he added.  \par \par \uc2 In contrast, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg said on Wednesday that euro zone interest rates are appropriate at 2 percent - indicating no cuts are expected in the foreseeable future - while inflation is on target, also at 2 percent, making the euro slightly more attractive.  \par \par \uc2 "Indications of an upturn in economic activity starting in the second half of the year have strengthened," Duisenberg told the Economic and Monetary Affairs Committee in Brussels in his last report before retiring at the end of October.  \par \par \uc2 Since last week, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shed almost 5 cents against the euro, retracing a substantial part of a rally that took it from record lows of $1.1932 against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June to a four-month peak of $1.0760 earlier this month.  \par \par \uc2 The euro fell to $1.1187 , off 0.35 percent, dropping from $1.1236 near Tuesday's three-week low of $1.1240.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the market is also mulling the longer-term impact of the rising U.S. budget deficit, which after U.S. President George W. Bush on Sunday asked Congress for an additional $87 billion, means it will exceed $500 billion.  \par \par \uc2 "In the last 10 days or so, the feeling the economy was doing better than thought and helped drive the dollar higher has shifted, especially after Mr. Bush's speech on the costs for Iraq, to paying more attention to deficit issues rather than growth strategies," said said John McCarthy, director of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at ING Capital Markets in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "We are going to have to get some awfully good economic data to grow our way out of these deficits. Otherwise I think you will start to see this twin deficit story exert more (selling) pressure on the dollar," he added.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling traded at $1.5908 , off a bit for the day.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN HOLDS DOLLAR AT BAY  \par \par \uc2 Despite the economic doubts, the dollar found support against the yen with traders suspicious of Bank of Japan dollar buying interest around 116.80-70 yen. In New York trade, the dollar rose to 117.15 yen , up 0.31 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The euro traded at 131.04 , off 0.07 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The persistent upward pressure on the yen, which has driven much of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  effort, was also boosted on Wednesday after data showed \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy growing at its fastest pace in two and a half years in the second quarter at 1 percent. The number was revised from the preliminary 0.6 percent reading.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said this may have driven the BOJ, which acts on behalf of the finance ministry in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, to step into the market again to protect export competitiveness from the painful effect of a strong yen.  \par \par \uc2 "The BOJ is leaning against the wind but it is becoming a pretty strong head-wind at this point," said Durrant.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030910dz9a003cr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
