\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hobbles near 3-wk low vs euro, BoJ watched.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Justyna Pawlak  \par \uc2 610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2003\par 12:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 10 (Reuters) - The dollar traded near a three week low on the euro on Wednesday, unable to shake off doubts about the pace of U.S. recovery, while hugging a tight range on the yen amid talk of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The dollar, which had rallied sharply from a record low set in June, has shed nearly five cents versus the euro over the past week. After weeks of optimism that a U.S. recovery was on the way, a poor jobs reading last Friday cast doubt over its sustainability.  \par \par \uc2 However, the dollar found support against the yen with traders in Tokyo and London suspicious of Bank of Japan dollar buying interest around 116.80-70 yen in order to prevent rises in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from damaging economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 "The market has just run out of dollar buyers. We have also reached a key technical level of $1.1240," said Steven Saywell, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank in London.  \par \par \uc2 "There is also a sense of disagreement over how long the economic recovery in the U.S. will last," he said.  \par \par \uc2 By 1020 GMT the dollar was firmer from the New York close at $1.1207 per euro, having fallen more than a cent from the start of trade on Tuesday and is well below a four-month peak of $1.0760 set last week.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback was trapped between 116.67-99 yen after falling to 116.10 in the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 In Sweden, where the country holds a referendum on Sunday on whether to join Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the crown fell to a two-week low against the strengthening euro as opinion polls showed euro opponents increased their lead over euro supporters.  \par \par \uc2 HESITANT MARKETS  \par \par \uc2 Data on Friday showed a sharp fall in U.S. non-farm payrolls in August and since then there has been no major or more forward-looking data to counter investor nerves that expectations for a U.S. recovery may have got ahead of themselves.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the market is also mulling the longer term impact of the rising budget deficit, which after U.S. President George W. Bush on Sunday asked Congress for an additional $87 billion, means it will exceed $500 billion.  \par \par \uc2 "The weak payrolls number last Friday has led ultimately to lower bond yields and a shakier equity markets," said Shahab Jalinoos, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in London.  \par \par \uc2 "There seems as well to be a lot of momentum driven speculative buying of euro/dollar. It's partly sparked by fundamental issues but there are also technical factors as well."  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN DOESN'T GIVE UP The persistent upward pressure on the yen, which has driven much of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  effort, was boosted on Wednesday after data showed \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  growing at its fastest pace in two and a half years in the second quarter of this year at one percent.  \par \par \uc2 The number was revised from the preliminary 0.6 percent reading.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said this may have driven the BoJ, which acts on behalf of the finance ministry in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, to step into the market again to protect export competitiveness from the painful effect of a strong yen.  \par \par \uc2 "We've detected strange (dollar) bids throughout the day, probably from the Japanese authorities," said a trader at a Japanese bank.  \par \par \uc2 The day's event include a testimony by U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow before the House Financial Services Committee at 1400 GMT and a testimony by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg before the European Parliament's Economic and Monetary Affairs Committee at 1300 GMT.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030910dz9a0028w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
