\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar limps, troubled by stocks, aided by BOJ.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Charlotte Cooper  \par \uc2 805 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 September 2003\par 10:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  LONDON, Sept 10 (Reuters) - The dollar limped along near a three week low on the euro, undermined by a weak close on Wall Street, and was hobbled in a tight range against the yen, as speculation of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stifled action.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar plunged 1-1/2 cents against the euro on Tuesday on falls on Wall Street, falling bond yields and concern after a poor jobs reading last week that the U.S. recovery may not live up to expectations.   \par \par \uc2  It found support against the yen however with traders in Tokyo and London suspicious of Bank of Japan dollar buying interest around 116.80-70 yen in order to prevent rises in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from damaging economic recovery.   \par \par \uc2  "The weak payrolls number last Friday has led ultimately to lower bond yields and a shakier equity markets," said Shahab Jalinoos, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in London.   \par \par \uc2  "There seems as well to be a lot of momentum driven speculative buying of euro/dollar. It's partly sparked by fundamental issues but there are also technical factors as well."   \par \par \uc2  By 0815 GMT the dollar was firmer from the New York close at $1.1204 per euro, having fallen more than a cent from the start of trade on Tuesday. It has had a sharp reversal of fortune since hitting a four-month peak at $1.0760 just last week.   \par \par \uc2  The greenback was trapped between 116.67-99 yen after falling to 116.10 in the previous session. Traders in New York cited talk of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the dollar gained sharply from the low, although there was no confirmation, and in Asian trade dealers said the dollar was supported by bids at 116.80.   \par \par \uc2  In Sweden, where the country holds a referendum on Sunday on whether to join Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the crown fell to a two-week low against the strengthening euro as opinion polls showed euro opponents increased their lead over euro supporters.   \par \par \uc2  WHY NOW?   \par \par \uc2  Data on Friday showed a sharp fall in U.S. non-farm payrolls in August and since then there has been no major or more forward-looking data to counter investor nerves that expectations for a U.S. recovery may have got ahead of themselves.   \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the market is also mulling the longer term impact of the rising budget deficit, which after U.S. President George W. Bush on Sunday asked Congress for an additional $87 billion, means it will exceed $500 billion.   \par \par \uc2  "People were long of dollars yesterday and got caught on the wrong side of the market," said Alex Patelis, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Merrill Lynch in London.   \par \par \uc2  "Technically it was a big day, with many \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  breaking through downward channels."   \par \par \uc2  Major U.S. indices ended around 0.8 percent lower on Tuesday after Finnish mobile phone giant Nokia warned of a possible fall in revenue, fuelling concerns that U.S. companies will report only slow growth in revenues in the third quarter.   \par \par \uc2  BOJ'S BATTLE   \par \par \uc2  In Japan, data showed that, for now, overseas demand for Japanese goods was healthy with the trade surplus up 7.1 percent in July from a year earlier and exports up 5.4 percent.   \par \par \uc2  Separate government data showed that real gross domestic product (GDP) grew 1.0 percent in the April-June quarter rather than the initial estimate of 0.6 percent as firms boosted capital spending to cope with the foreign demand.   \par \par \uc2  Traders said non-Japanese fund operators' demand for the yen increased after the GDP data. Japanese exporters were also actively looking for chances to sell the dollar against the yen ahead of first-half book-closings at the end of the month.   \par \par \uc2  "We've detected strange (dollar) bids throughout the day, probably from the Japanese authorities," said a trader at a Japanese bank.   \par \par \uc2  "There have been plenty of sell orders from exporters and foreigners since early trade so the dollar remains under downward pressure, but it was unnaturally pegged around 116.80 yen," he added.   \par \par \uc2  In addition, Finance Ministry data showed foreigners bought 1.5982 trillion yen of Japanese shares in August, not far off the July figure of 1.7070 trillion yen and the third month in a row it has topped one trillion.   \par \par \uc2  Given such yen-buying pressure, many traders think \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  further if it wants to hold the line at 115 yen to the dollar. Analysts say a break below that level could see the dollar drop sharply.   \par \par \uc2  The day's event include a testimony by U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow before the House Financial Services Committee at 1400 GMT and a testimony by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg before the European Parliament's Economic and Monetary Affairs Committee at 1300 GMT.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030910dz9a001s6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
