\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar rebounds vs yen - traders cite Japan buying.  \b0\par\par\uc2 372 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 September 2003\par 20:33\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 9 (Reuters) - The dollar rebounded against the yen in afternoon New York trade on Tuesday, with dealers suspecting that the Bank of Japan had asked Japanese banks to sell yen on its behalf.  \par \par \uc2 Amid talk of orders to buy dollars near 116.10 yen, the dollar snapped quickly higher from session lows around 116.12 yen, to 116.95 yen , trading slightly higher on the day. The greenback by contrast remained sharply lower against a bevy of other major counterparts.  \par \par \uc2 New York traders had not seen the BoJ bidding for dollars, but cited Japanese commercial banks in the market, which the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  often asks to buy dollars and euros for it.  \par \par \uc2 "There's talk of (yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) and there's no confirmation yet but it seems most of the buying was done out of Tokyo," said Thomas Molloy, a trader with Bank Leumi in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "Down around 116.10 yen is where the Tokyo bid was noticed everywhere," Molloy said. "Whether it's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or not, it certainly looks like it," Molloy added.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials in New York could not be immediately reached for comment. When \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  do occur, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's BoJ acts on behalf of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which conducts the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 Separately, there was very strong demand for four-week Treasury bills at an auction that took place, when results were announced early on Tuesday afternoon. Short-dated Treasury instruments are a favorite parking spot for any purchases of dollars by central banks.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's government is concerned with checking the appreciation of the yen in order to protect the competitiveness of Japanese exporters and nurture signs of a tentative recovery in the world's second biggest economy.  \par \par \uc2 "We had not seen (the BoJ); we heard of some buying from Japanese commercial banks," said Scott Schultz, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with Brown Brothers Harriman in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "Reportedly, there were bids out of Tokyo," between 116.25 and 116.10 yen, Schultz added.  \par \par \uc2 After staying its hand in August, the BoJ has returned to yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the past few days, according to traders.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030909dz99003fn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
