\par\par \b \uc2  Dollar Falls Against Yen Despite Apparent  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2  By Jamie McGeever and Tom Barkley \par \uc2 576 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2003\par 22:51\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- Another round of apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to stem the yen's appreciation Monday, with the dollar ending New York trading weaker against the yen. \par \par \uc2  Aided by a rally in U.S. stocks, however, the greenback recouped some of the ground lost in Friday's heavy selloff to eke out gains against the euro and sterling. \par \par \uc2  But in a session devoid of major economic-data releases and other market-moving events, attention was focused on the dollar's moves against the yen, especially after the Bank of Japan -- on behalf of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- reportedly entered the market during Australian trading hours, buying around $5 billion of dollars for yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar immediately strengthened to a session high of around 117.65 yen from 116.80 yen, but it failed to hold the gains, holding below that level for much of New York trading and actually ending the day below there. The drop shows just how heavily the market is betting on further yen strength, and underscores the huge task Japanese authorities are facing if they want to stop the rampaging yen from derailing a nascent export-led economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  Certainly, if market positioning is anything to go by, Japanese authorities are in for a tough battle. \par \par \uc2  As of a week ago, the speculative-trading community on the International Monetary Market on the Chicago \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -futures exchange held a huge net-long yen position of around 47,000 contracts. That was the largest in four years and the second highest on record, showing just how much those investors are betting on a stronger yen. \par \par \uc2  "I'd say there [is] still a large short dollar/yen position on the IMM," said Greg Anderson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in Chicago. "I don't think [the BOJ] hit the stops," which would have triggered massive unwinding of those large positions. \par \par \uc2  In late trading in New York, the dollar was at 116.71 yen, down from 116.83 yen late Friday and well off its session high of 117.68 yen. The euro was at $1.1064, down from $1.1107 late Friday in New York. The pound fell to $1.5817 from $1.5900. \par \par \uc2  To be sure, the short-term market's extreme positioning may also play into the Bank of Japan's hands. If the Japanese monetary authorities can show they really mean business in their yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market activities, the speculators might decide it is too risky holding long yen positions and liquidate them. \par \par \uc2  A senior dealer at a Japanese bank in New York reckons the dollar will have to strengthen to around 118.50 yen before forcing the IMM accounts to unwind those trades. \par \par \uc2  In the other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the euro touched a session high Monday of $1.1130 -- its strongest level since Aug. 20 -- but failed to hold the gains, dragged lower by selling against the yen. That said, a weak U.S. labor-market report last Friday and dovish comments from Federal Reserve officials last Thursday are providing the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  with some support. "The continued short-term bias for the euro is higher," said Bob Lynch, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in New York. \par \par \uc2  -By Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  09-08-03 1651ET \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030908dz98004h5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
