\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Speculators Still Heavily Long Yen Despite BOJ \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jamie McGeever \par \uc2 821 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2003\par 19:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Speculative \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are still betting heavily that the dollar will weaken against the yen, despite the Bank of Japan's best efforts to the contrary, market participants say. \par \par \uc2  The latest positioning data from the International Monetary Market on the Chicago \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  futures exchange show traders held net long yen positions of 46,955 contracts in the week ending Sept 2. \par \par \uc2  This level - a four-year high and the second largest on record - is likely to have been reduced following the BOJ's reported bouts of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday and Friday last week, and earlier Monday. But not by much, observers say. \par \par \uc2  "I'd say there was still a large short dollar/yen position on the IMM as of (last) Friday," said Greg Anderson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in Chicago. "I don't think they (BOJ) hit the stops," which would have triggered massive unwinding of these large positions. \par \par \uc2  The data suggest the Japanese government has a formidable task on its hands in seeking to tame the rampaging yen and limit the damage to a nascent, export-led economic recovery. The IMM report also highlights the fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - under which it mostly buys dollars for yen - doesn't appear to be having the classic effect of flushing speculators, those who take a shorter-term view of trading, out of the markets. \par \par \uc2  The IMM net long yen position could still be around two thirds of last week's extreme level, Anderson predicted. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has already spent about $80 billion this year in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and reportedly stepped into the market again last Thursday when the dollar was around Y116.40 and on Friday when it fell to Y115.75. Earlier in Monday's global session, the BOJ bought dollar and sold yen when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at Y116.80, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The moves succeeded in prompting some covering of short positions and lifting the dollar higher, by as much as a full yen. But not high enough to force many of these IMM accounts to take profit and liquidate their long yen trades. In fact, by early afternoon in New York, the dollar was back below the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels, changing hands at Y116.60, down from Y116.83 late Friday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par         Funds Flowing Into Equities\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The (speculative) interest is still to short dollar/yen," said the\par chief dealer of a Japanese bank in New York. In his opinion, the dollar\par would have to rise to around Y118.50 - BOJ-assisted or otherwise - to\par prompt a sizable reduction in the IMM positioning.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par At the moment, that appears an unlikely prospect, unless the BOJ\par becomes even more aggressive. Dealers are betting on Asian export-driven\par economies being best placed to profit from a U.S.-led global economic\par recovery. Money is pouring into the region's equity markets -\par particularly Japan's Nikkei - and Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are appreciating\par against the dollar, despite the efforts of local central banks to guard\par against that.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Officially, there was no \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the BOJ in August, but\par already in September, it has spent in excess of $10 billion, according\par to some market estimates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, IMM traders are still thought relatively bearish on\par European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the dollar, even after the sharp gains made\par Friday by the euro, Swiss franc and sterling.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The latest data showed IMM dealers net short of  Swiss francs to the\par tune of just over 20,000 contracts, the largest in over two years. This\par compared with another reduced net long euro position to 2,129 contracts,\par and a small net short sterling position of 2,325 contracts.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par `It's my hunch that the (IMM) market's still short of European\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said ABN Amro's Anderson.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Friday, following weak U.S. job market data, the dollar sold off\par heavily against most \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , notably the euro, Swiss franc and\par sterling. The August payrolls report - which showed that companies shed\par 93,000 jobs in the month compared with expectations of a 12,000 rise -\par was so surprisingly weak that near-term sentiment toward the dollar\par definitely appears to have soured.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Citibank, one of the largest \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market participants and\par dollar bulls, cited the reduced euro long position as one of the factors\par behind its decision to close out long dollar positions against the euro\par and Swiss franc.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par But the upside for the Swiss franc may be limited, because of the\par attractive interest rate play offered by taking short positions on the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Swiss benchmark interest rates are targeted at 0.25% over three\par months, so dealers can borrow in Swiss francs and earn interest of 2% by\par investing in euros, or even 1% if they buy dollars.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938-2096;\par jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030908dz98006y1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
