\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Data vacuum sets dollar adrift -  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  wanes.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \uc2 663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2003\par 19:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 8 (Reuters) - The dollar gave up early gains on Monday, unable to sustain a two-week high against the yen after Japanese authorities were cited once again selling their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the greenback.  \par \par \uc2 Initially, the dollar began the week by gaining ground, a contrast to Friday's activity, when a dismal U.S. jobs report sparked a sell-off. But a dearth of economic data left investors to lick their wounds and plan their strategy.  \par \par \uc2 "The jobs report has changed people's expectations about speed of the recovery, and any change in future interest rate policy may be delayed. These factors combined, point to a bit of a weaker dollar in the medium-term," said Patrick Brodie, senior trader at SMBC in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Brodie referred to expectations on benchmark U.S. interest rates. Rising rates in response to economic growth would make the dollar more attractive to foreign investors. However, the latest Reuters poll of Wall Street's primary bond dealers showed the majority surveyed expect rates will not rise until at least mid-2004.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar drifted near 116.59 yen , a small loss from Friday's New York close. The Euro fell to 129.68 yen , off slightly on the day. Earlier, the euro reached a near two-week high of 130.62 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Trading sources said it appeared Japanese authorities sold yen to buy between $2 billion and $4 billion. Japan would not confirm its presence in the market.  \par \par \uc2 "Back in action - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely believed to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again today, with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected right at the Tokyo open," wrote Anne Parker Mills, senior U.S. economist with Brown Brothers Harriman in New York, in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts also noted Friday's data showing speculators made the biggest bet in four years that the dollar would fall against the yen as a possible reason for Japan to step into the market with yen selling last week - and on Monday as well.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market intermittently since the middle of last week after its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose sharply on news Tokyo had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all in August.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Toshihiko Fukui declined to comment on the yen on Monday when questioned by reporters as he arrived at the G10 central bankers' meeting.  \par \par \uc2 However, Japanese Vice Finance Minister Masakazu Hayashi said on Monday: "When the market makes rapid, big moves due to speculative trade, we will actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." But Hayashi did not say any specific action had been taken.  \par \par \uc2 The speculative position was built up ahead of U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow's recent trip through Asia, where he repeated the U.S. stance that markets should determine \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be kept to a minimum.  \par \par \uc2 "To me it is clear they (BOJ) were trying to clear out stops on the IMM positions. But my guess is they probably didn't clear them out because they are above the 118 yen level," said Greg Anderson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO in Chicago.  \par \par \uc2 EURO PULLS EVEN  \par \par \uc2 The euro rebounded from its lowest levels in more than two weeks, rising from a low of $1.1047 to trade at $1.1106 , unchanged from Friday's New York close.  \par \par \uc2 Incoming European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet, speaking as chairman of a G10 meeting of central bankers in Switzerland, said bankers were encouraged by improvements in the global economy, although risks remain. They expected European growth to disappoint in 2003 but saw a pickup in the pipeline.  \par \par \uc2 The euro showed no reaction to his statement or news that Germany's draft 2004 budget text showed a rising risk that growth next year would fall short of the 2 percent forecast.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was still vulnerable after Friday's nasty jobless surprise, which showed U.S. non-farm payrolls falling instead of a forecast rise.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030908dz980038y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
