\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar buoyed vs yen on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  talk.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \uc2 688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2003\par 17:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comment, changes byline, changes dateline; previous LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 8 (Reuters) - The dollar strengthened on Monday, clawing back from Friday's drubbing on a dismal U.S. jobs report and gaining support on talk that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak heading into the end of the quarter.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit a two-week high of 117.71 yen at the Tokyo open before pulling back.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar drifted near 116.78 yen , just above Friday's New York close. The Euro fell to 129.26 yen , off 0.38 percent on the day. Earlier, the euro reached a near two-week high of 130.62 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Trading sources said it appeared Japanese authorities sold yen to buy between $2 billion and $4 billion. Japan would not confirm its presence in the market.  \par \par \uc2 With little U.S. economic data in the pipeline until Friday's August retail sales report, participants expect a quiet trading environment.  \par \par \uc2 "Back in action - The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely believed to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again today, with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected right at the Tokyo open," wrote Anne Parker Mills, senior U.S. economist with Brown Brothers Harriman in New York, in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts also noted Friday's data showing speculators made the biggest bet in four years that the dollar would fall against the yen as a possible reason for Japan to step into the market with yen selling last week - and on Monday as well.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market intermittently since the middle of last week after its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose sharply on news Tokyo had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all in August.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Toshihiko Fukui declined to comment on the yen on Monday when he was questioned by reporters as he arrived at the G10 central bankers' meeting.  \par \par \uc2 However, Japanese Vice Finance Minister Masakazu Hayashi said on Monday: "When the market makes rapid, big moves due to speculative trade, we will actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." But Hayashi did not say any specific action had been taken.  \par \par \uc2 The speculative position was built up ahead of U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow's recent trip through Asia, where he repeated the U.S. stance that markets should determine \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates and \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be kept to a minimum.  \par \par \uc2 "To me it is clear they (BOJ) were trying to clear out stops on the IMM positions. But my guess is they probably didn't clear them out because they are above the 118 yen level," said Greg Anderson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO in Chicago.  \par \par \uc2 NO GOOD NEWS FOR EURO  \par \par \uc2 Incoming European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet, speaking as chairman of a G10 meeting of central bankers in Switzerland, said bankers were encouraged by improvements in the global economy, although risks remain. They expected European growth to disappoint in 2003 but saw a pickup in the pipeline.  \par \par \uc2 The euro showed no reaction to his statement or news that Germany's draft 2004 budget text showed a rising risk that growth next year would fall short of the 2 percent forecast.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was still vulnerable after Friday's nasty jobless surprise, which showed U.S. non-farm payrolls falling instead of a forecast rise.  \par \par \uc2 It was up a third of a percent on the euro at $1.1068 , not far from its lowest levels in more than two weeks, which it hit on Friday in the wake of the worse-than-expected U.S. jobless data.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was not moved by U.S. President George W. Bush's plans to ask Congress for $87 billion more in anti-terrorism funds to pay for \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Iraq and Afghanistan.  \par \par \uc2 But analysts said the vast deficit, likely to exceed half a trillion dollars for the first time, underscored future pressures for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "Over the long term, this is not good news. It builds pressure on the dollar. But today, this was not a big surprise," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030908dz98002s2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
