\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen claws back after talk of  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Burton Frierson  \par \uc2 759 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2003\par 14:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 8 (Reuters) - The yen steadied against the dollar on Monday after dropping to its lowest for nearly two weeks on talk that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to cap its strength and protect domestic exports.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile the euro cut its losses against the dollar, edging back toward Friday's two-week highs hit on the back of dismal U.S. jobs data, but had difficulty pressing ahead further.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said a lack of major data early this week left markets few signposts to decide whether the dollar's fall on Friday was a change of direction or just a pause, while suspicions Tokyo was softening the yen also gave it pause for thought.  \par \par \uc2 "Friday's U.S. payrolls were a big surprise. The question now is does the downtrend, in play on euro/dollar since the middle of June, end here. Unfortunately there isn't going to be much data this week to add to that picture," said Shahab Jalinoos, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro.  \par \par \uc2 "In dollar/yen, the Bank of Japan has been fairly successful until now in trying to stem the decline. It's been helped somewhat by the fact that the U.S. data was weak, mainly because Japan is seen as a leveraged play on global growth."  \par \par \uc2 By 1225 GMT, the yen was fairly flat on the day at 116.87 per dollar, after making good progress from the day's weakest level of 117.71, its worst since August 27.  \par \par \uc2 It fell sharply from around 116.80 to 117.70 in early Tokyo hours, sparking talk that the Bank of Japan was in the market selling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for dollars.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was up a third of a percent on the euro at $1.1070, but still not far from its lowest levels in more than two weeks, which it hit on Friday in the wake of the worse than expected U.S. jobless data.  \par \par \uc2 Against the euro, the yen was firmer on the day at 129.33, having dipped to its lowest in around two weeks at 130.62 earlier.  \par \par \uc2 NO GOOD NEWS FOR EURO  \par \par \uc2 Future European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet, speaking as chairman of a G10 meeting of central bankers in Switzerland, said they were encouraged by improvements in the global economy, although risks remain. They expected European growth to disappoint in 2003 but saw a pickup in the pipeline.  \par \par \uc2 The euro showed no reaction to this or news that Germany's draft 2004 budget text showed a rising risk that growth next year would fall short of the two percent forecast.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was still vulnerable after Friday's nasty jobless surprise, which showed U.S. non-farm payrolls falling instead of forecasts for a rise.  \par \par \uc2 It was not moved by U.S. President George W. Bush's newly announced spending plans, but analysts said the vast deficit underscored future pressures for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Bush asked Congress on Sunday night for $87 billion for the U.S. military and reconstruction in Iraq, meaning the overall budget deficit is likely to exceed half a trillion dollars for the first time.  \par \par \uc2 "Over the long term, this is not good news. It builds pressure on the dollar, but today this was not a big surprise," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez.  \par \par \uc2 Later on Monday, the Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City releases its August manufacturing survey at 1500 GMT. In July the survey stood at 8.0 but no Reuters forecast is available for this month.  \par \par \uc2 The key data release of the week is likely to be U.S. retail sales on Friday, which are expected to rise for a fourth month in a row, plumped by an August car-buying spree.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  SUSPICIONS REIGNITED  \par \par \uc2 Dealers were suspicious that Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in very early Asian trade, but Japanese officials did not confirm their presence in the market.  \par \par \uc2 "I think there was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight," said Kotecha.  \par \par \uc2 "It is interesting that they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  when dollar/yen was not under pressure, showing that 117 (yen per dollar) is really a key level."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market intermittently since the middle of last week after its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose sharply on news Tokyo had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all in August.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Toshihiko Fukui declined to comment on the yen on Monday, when he was questioned by reporters as he arrived at the G10 central bankers' meeting.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030908dz98001uu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
