\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Japan yen selling meant to ease pressure-Fukui.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuko Waki   \par \uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2003\par 14:30\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BASEL, Switzerland, Sept 8 (Reuters) - Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said on Monday \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets is meant to alleviate market pressure and that movements in the yen have stabilised recently.   \par \par \uc2 Speaking on the sidelines of a bi-monthly meeting of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors at the Bank for International Settlements, Fukui also said the BOJ would continue its current easing measures and would not discuss any tightening at this week's meeting.   \par \par \uc2 "(Our \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) is meant to alleviate market pressure, not to guide markets. The yen movements have rather stabilised so no problem for the time being. Really, it must be consistent with the future path of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recovery," Fukui told reporters.   \par \par \uc2 "(Our \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy) is in line with the direction of our present easing measures."   \par \par \uc2 Japan spent a record nine trillion yen to curb the export-damaging strength of the yen in the first seven months of this year. Signs of a recovery in the world's second biggest economy have boosted flows into Japanese stocks, driving the yen to three-month highs of 115.73 per dollar last week.   \par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy has become a thorny political issue following U.S. calls for Asia to allow its heavily protected \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise.   \par \par \uc2 Washington is under domestic pressure to stem the loss of U.S. manufacturing jobs in the run-up to the 2004 presidential election. Manufacturing is an important political and fund-raising base for President George W. Bush.   \par \par \uc2 The United States is also seen keen to raise the issue of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a meeting of finance ministers from Group of Seven leading industrial nations in Dubai later this month.   \par \par \uc2 Earlier on Monday People's Bank of China Vice Governor Wu Xiaoling said she saw no possibility of yuan revaluation in the foreseeable future and the level of the yuan was suitable for China's growth.   \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Korea's Assistant Governor Lee Jae-ouk also said the bank is prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out any abrupt moves in the market and said there was no pressure on Asia from Washington or internationally to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise.   \par \par \uc2 NO TIGHTENING YET   \par \par \uc2 Under its ultra-easy monetary policy, introduced in March 2001, the BOJ floods the money market to keep interest rates near zero. It buys 1.2 trillion yen of long-term Japanese Government Bonds per month to boost credit market liquidity.   \par \par \uc2 But Fukui was cautious about the outlook for Japan's recovery and said there would be no discussion of scrapping the current quantitative easing measures.   \par \par \uc2 "We are still cautious about the outlook for Japan's economy, but there are certainly some good signs," he said.   \par \par \uc2 "The BOJ's basic stance is to continue current easing measures. There will be absolutely no possibility of the BOJ discussing tightening in this week's meeting."   \par \par \uc2 When asked about possible plans to increase the amount of JGB purchases, Fukui said: "There are no plans at the moment."   \par \par \uc2 YIELD STABILISING TOO   \par \par \uc2 Recent data showing signs of a recovery have triggered a sell-off in JGB markets, with the benchmark yield nearly quadrupling in the last few months from a record low of 0.43 percent hit in June.   \par \par \uc2 Rising long-term interest rates in Japan have fanned concerns that banks may take losses on bond holdings and companies may face higher funding costs.   \par \par \uc2 But Fukui said: "Markets fluctuate, but they are not on course for violent swings. They are trying to stabilise."   \par \par \uc2 Asked about the U.S. economy, he said: "I hope the U.S. economy will recover strongly because that's the most important issue in the global economy. The U.S. current account deficit is a long-term issue."    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030908dz980012x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
