\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Yen down but not out on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  talk.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Justyna Pawlak  \par \uc2 693 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2003\par 10:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Sept 8 (Reuters) - The yen kept to its lowest level on the dollar in almost two weeks on Monday on talk that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets earlier in the session to cap its strength and protect domestic exports.  \par \par \uc2 However, Japanese officials did not confirm their presence in the market and the yen regained some strength in early European hours.  \par \par \uc2 The euro stepped off Friday's two-week highs versus the dollar, which had been hit by disappointing labour data from the United States.  \par \par \uc2 "I think there was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight," said Mitul Kotecha, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Credit Agricole Indosuez in London.  \par \par \uc2 "It is interesting that they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  when dollar/yen was not under pressure, showing that 117 (yen per dollar) is really a key level."  \par \par \uc2 At 0800 GMT, the yen was trading at 117.10 per dollar , down 0.30 percent from Friday's close.  \par \par \uc2 It fell sharply from around 116.80 to some 117.70 in early Tokyo hours, sparking talk that the Bank of Japan was in the market selling the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for dollars.  \par \par \uc2 Against the euro, the yen was steady on the day at 129.60 , having dipped to its lowest in around two weeks at 130.62 earlier.  \par \par \uc2 The euro also gained a third of a percent on the dollar to $1.1069 . It is now around nearly nine cents below record highs set in May and June.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  SUSPICIONS REIGNITED  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market intermittently since the middle of last week after its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rose sharply on news Tokyo had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all in August.  \par \par \uc2 The yen's most substantial drop on Monday took place around 6:30 a.m. in Tokyo (2130 GMT, Sunday), and dealers said it was unusual for the authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at such hours.  \par \par \uc2 But analysts expect the yen to remain under upward pressure despite the authorities' efforts to muzzle its rise out of concern that appreciation will damage economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 In the near term, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be supported by a strong stock market, improving economic data and the widely expected reelection of Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi as the leader of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party later this month.  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei average closed up 0.31 percent on Monday and was near its highest levels in more than a year hit last week.  \par \par \uc2 Kosuke Hanao, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at the Royal Bank of Scotland, said the yen would stay strong in the next few weeks, though its rise would be limited.  \par \par \uc2 "If the BOJ's (Bank of Japan) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is unchanged, it should stay around 115-120 (to the dollar)," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "(But) October is a new month. Koizumi should be reelected, and it will be the beginning of the second half of the fiscal year. In this circumstance we can have a little more yen appreciation."  \par \par \uc2 DOLLAR STUMBLES  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was still suffering from wounds inflicted by a disappointing jobs report which showed U.S non-farm payrolls falling 93,000 versus forecasts for a 12,000 rise.  \par \par \uc2 It was not moved by U.S. President George W. Bush's newly announced spending plans, but analysts said the vast deficit underscored future pressures for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "Over the long term, this is not good news. It builds pressure on the dollar, but today this was not a big surprise," said Kotecha.  \par \par \uc2 Bush asked Congress on Sunday night for $87 billion for the U.S. military and reconstruction in Iraq. The figure was at the high end of expectations and means the overall budget deficit is likely to exceed half a trillion dollars for the first time.  \par \par \uc2 Also on Monday, the Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City was due to release its August manufacturing survey at 1500 GMT. In July the survey stood at 8.0 but no Reuters forecast is available for this month.  \par \par \uc2 Central bankers from the Group of 10 leading economies were meeting in Basel and future European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Jean-Claude Trichet was to brief media later on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030908dz98000yl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
