\par\par \b \uc2 ASIA MARKETS-Yen down on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  talk, Tokyo stocks gain.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Richard Baum  \par \uc2 625 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2003\par 04:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 8 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  appeared to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen on Monday after poor U.S. jobs data undermined the dollar, traders said, but hopes for better news on the Japanese economy lifted Tokyo shares.   \par \par \uc2  An index of stocks elsewhere in the Asian-Pacific region continued to touch levels unseen since early 2001, led by gains in technology shares and despite losses in Hong Kong and Seoul.   \par \par \uc2  The yen slipped nearly one percent against the dollar and euro in early trade amid persistent talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities.   \par \par \uc2  But it soon began to creep back up in the absence of official confirmation or further evidence of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action.   \par \par \uc2  "It seems the Bank of Japan has bought (dollars) this morning in thin market conditions," said a senior manager at a Japanese trust bank. "It's a bit unnatural to see such a big spike around this time of the day."   \par \par \uc2  The yen fell towards 117.75 to the dollar compared with 116.80 in late New York trade on Friday and 3-1/2-month highs around 115.75 set last week. But by 0225 GMT, the yen had clawed back up to around 117.35 to the dollar.   \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also fell beyond 130 to the euro for the first time in more than two weeks. The euro was steady against the dollar at around $1.11 holding onto gains made on Friday on the weak U.S. jobs data.   \par \par \uc2  The jobs report showed non-farm payrolls fell 93,000 versus forecasts for a 12,000 rise, while the jobless rate declined to 6.1 percent from 6.2 percent, when no change was expected.   \par \par \uc2  ASIAN STOCKS MIXED   \par \par \uc2  That weakened U.S. stocks, with a knock-on effect on shares in Hong Kong, South Korea and Singapore Benchmark indices for all three markets were around 0.3 percent weaker.   \par \par \uc2  But Tokyo's Nikkei average ended morning trade up half a percent at 10,707.83 and an MSCI index of shares elsewhere in Asia Pacific climbed a quarter of a percent, led by its technology component.   \par \par \uc2  Japanese shares moved back towards 14-month peaks on hopes that a string of data this week will reinforce signs of a brighter outlook for the economy.   \par \par \uc2  That optimism was reflected in gains in bank shares such as Sumitomo Mitsui Financial Group, which rose 4.5 percent.   \par \par \uc2  Traders are looking forward to a batch of economic data this week, with July machinery orders, a gauge of capital spending, on Tuesday, followed by revised gross domestic product (GDP) data for April-June on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2  "There's a very good chance that GDP data will be revised up this week, which could prove the trigger for a charge towards 11,000 by the Nikkei," said Koji Muneoka, head of domestic sales trading at HSBC Securities.   \par \par \uc2  Sony Corp, the world's biggest consumer electronics firm, rose three percent, while office equipment and camera giant Canon Inc was up almost two percent.   \par \par \uc2  Taiwan rose 0.5 percent, helped by gains of more than two percent in Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co and United Microelectronics Corp Australia advanced a third of a percent.   \par \par \uc2  Despite the weak jobs report, Wall Street strategists expect U.S. stocks to gain in coming days following positive economic data in recent weeks and a bullish revenue forecast from technology bellwether Intel Corp   \par \par \uc2  The jobs data provided indirect support for Japanese government bond (JGB) prices, which jumped across the board after U.S. Treasuries surged on the weak data.   \par \par \uc2  The key September 10-year JGB futures contract hit its highest level since August 27, traders said.   \par \par \uc2  Oil gained and gold weakened.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030908dz98000by\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
