\par\par \b \uc2 Yen falls on  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  talk, but trims losses.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima  \par \uc2 611 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 September 2003\par 03:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Sept 8 (Reuters) - The yen slipped against the dollar on Monday amid persistent talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities to halt a recent rise in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Still, no official confirmation was available and the yen soon began to creep back up in the absence of any further evidence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tweaking.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar/yen rate basically depends on the Japanese government - whether \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is strong or not," said Mitsuru Saito, chief economist at UFJ Tsubasa Securities.  \par \par \uc2 Although the dollar broadly fell on Friday after a weaker-than-expected U.S. jobs report, its losses against the yen were limited due to anticipation that Japanese policymakers would want to stop a higher yen from choking \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's export-led economic recovery.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market intermittently since the middle of last week after the yen rose sharply on news that Tokyo had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all in August.  \par \par \uc2 The yen's latest drop took place around 6:30 a.m. in Tokyo (2130 GMT, Sunday), and dealers said it was unusual for the authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at such hours, particularly on a Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "It seems the Bank of Japan has bought (dollars) this morning in thin market conditions. It's a bit unnatural to see such a big spike around this time of the day," said a senior manager at a Japanese trust bank.  \par \par \uc2 The yen fell towards 117.75 to the dollar compared with 116.80 late in New York trade on Friday and 3-1/2-month highs around 115.75 set last week. But by 0050 GMT, the yen had clawed back up to around 117.30 to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also fell beyond 130 to the euro for the first time in more than two weeks.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was steady against the dollar at around $1.11 holding on to gains it had made on Friday on the weak U.S. jobs data.  \par \par \uc2 The jobs report showed non-farm payrolls fell 93,000 versus forecasts for a 12,000 rise, while the jobless rate declined to 6.1 percent from 6.2 percent, when no change was expected.  \par \par \uc2 NIKKEI STILL IN FOCUS  \par \par \uc2 The yen's rebound after initial losses came hand in hand with a bounce in the Tokyo stock market's Nikkei average, which fell nearly one percent soon after the open but erased most of the losses by mid-morning.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said capital flows were likely to favour a stronger yen in the near term, with the Nikkei outperforming most of its main rival indices abroad and a recent volatility in the bond market making Japanese investors wary of buying foreign bonds.  \par \par \uc2 "If it were not for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - or all the talk about it - supply and demand in the market points towards lower dollar/yen levels," said a dealer at a second Japanese trust bank.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan figures showing a marginal increase in Japan's money supply and continued decline in bank lending had negligible effect on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Japan's most widely watched measure of money supply - M2 plus certificates of deposit (CDs) - rose 2.0 percent in August from a year earlier, compared with a rise of 1.8 percent in July.  \par \par \uc2 Lending by Japan's main banks fell 5.2 percent in August, the 68th consecutive month of decline, to 399.41 trillion yen ($3.39 trillion).  \par \par \uc2 The economic data calendar for Monday was relatively light. But the market was expected to keep a close eye on any remarks from the Group of 10 \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chiefs' meeting in Basel.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030908dz98000ac\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
