\par\par \b \uc2  INTERVIEW:Japan Business Leader Says MOF  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Moves Proper \b0\par\par\uc2  By Ron Harui \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 649 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 2003\par 11:20\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent massive yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market were "appropriate" because they have prevented an excessive yen appreciation from hurting Japanese companies, a top Japanese business leader said Friday. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  above Y115. I think this is a proper decision from the viewpoint of curbing a sharp yen rise," said Kakutaro Kitashiro, chairman of the Japan Association of Corporate Executives, or Keizai Doyukai. \par \par \uc2  Just Thursday, the BOJ, acting on behalf of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , bought as much as $5 billion to prevent the dollar from sinking below the psychologically key Y115 level, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Kitashiro said that rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements such as a dollar plunge from Y115 to Y100 would have a huge negative impact on Japanese companies. \par \par \uc2  "Overswings of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are undesirable for corporate earnings," he told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. \par \par \uc2  Big fluctuations in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates also adversely affect Japanese corporate decisions on business investments as well as sites for production facilities, Kitashiro said. \par \par \uc2  "I think \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at curbing the overshooting of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates is OK," he said. Kitashiro added, however, that in general \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should be determined by market forces. \par \par \uc2  Concerned that an overly strong yen could hamper exports, one of the few bright spots behind Japan's nascent economic recovery, Japanese authorities have sold more than Y9 trillion in the first seven months of the year. \par \par \uc2  Indeed, a weak yen contributes to Japan's exports as well as to the nation's economic rebound, Kitashiro said. "A weaker yen is desirable for Japanese exporters." \par \par \uc2  Kitashiro said he expects the dollar to trade between Y115 and Y120 this year. \par \par \uc2  Around 0900 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y116.81. \par \par \uc2  Kitashiro, who is also chairman of IBM Japan Ltd., said that he is "cautiously watching" the Japanese government bond market for any further sudden rises in long-term interest rates after a recent rapid increase in yields on 10-year JGBs. \par \par \uc2  After two waves of selling that started in late June, the yield on new 10-year JGBs has soared to as high as 1.675% this week from a record low 0.43% in mid-June. The yield closed Friday at 1.44%. \par \par \uc2  "A sharp rise (in long-term interest rates) is undesirable," Kitashiro said. "It has a bad effect on corporations' business activities." \par \par \uc2  While 10-year JGB yields around 1.6% aren't worrisome, a rapid rise beyond that level would be a problem, he said, because that would have a negative impact on housing loans, capital investments and the government's interest-rate payments on its huge outstanding public debt, estimated to be around Y700 trillion. \par \par \uc2  "The Bank of Japan is taking steps to ward off sharp rises in long-term interest rates. (Those measures) are appropriate," Kitashiro said. \par \par \uc2  Turning to China, Kitashiro said the Chinese government should adopt a more flexible policy on the yuan by widening the trading band on the exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  "It should allow the market to set the value of the yuan," he said. \par \par \uc2  China has been under intense pressure to take a more flexible approach on its strict exchange rate regime that virtually pegs the yuan to the U.S. dollar. Some trading partners, particularly Japan, have criticized Beijing for intentionally holding down the value of the yuan. \par \par \uc2  China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is only fully convertible on the current account and even then it is kept in a tight band of around 8.28 yuan to the U.S. dollar. Trade at the Shanghai exchange is kept within a 0.3% range on either side of that value, effectively pegging the yuan to the dollar. \par \par \uc2  -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2942; ron.harui@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  -Edited by George B. Thomas \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020030905dz95000lv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
