\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Stocks Creep Still Higher \b0\par\par\uc2 1,684 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 2003\par 06:56\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: Economic Data, Intel Cheer Investors\par DJIA          9587.90    gain 19.44     up 0.2%\par NASDAQ        1868.97    gain 16.07     up 0.9%\par S&P 500       1027.97    gain 1.70      up 0.2%\par Dow Future    9580.00    loss 9.00      dn 0.1%\par NASDAQ Future 1377.50    gain 2.50      up 0.2%\par S&P Future    1028.00    loss 0.50         0.0%\par 10-Yr US Treasury: 4.50% down 0.07\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- The Nasdaq composite index notched its longest winning streak in more than three years Thursday after strong gains in factory orders and worker productivity fueled investor expectations of a rebounding economy. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS: Buying accelerated in the afternoon despite an unexpected rise in jobless claims. Analysts said investors remained upbeat and were willing to make additional modest bets on the market - particularly in tech stocks - even after several days of rallies. \par \par \uc2  "It's hard to doubt the economy is just going to get stronger and stronger," said Peter Dunay, chief market strategist at Wall Street Access, a New York-based brokerage firm. "Productivity numbers are very positive. Factory orders are picking up. Retail sales continue to be strong." \par \par \uc2  The Commerce Department reported Thursday that U.S. factory orders rose 1.6% in July, boosted by broad-based demand for big-ticket goods as well as nondurables such as chemicals. Analysts were expecting a more modest 0.8% gain. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, the Labor Department reported that U.S. productivity rose at an annual rate of 6.8% in the second quarter. The reading beat analysts' expectations of a 6.4% growth rate. \par \par \uc2  But the department said new jobless claims jumped last week by a seasonally adjusted 15,000 to 413,000. It was the highest level since the middle of July; it also stood above 400,000, a level associated with a weak job market. \par \par \uc2  Intel Corp.'s move Thursday to raise the low end of its third-quarter revenue projection further fueled optimism that the battered technology markets are recovering. \par \par \uc2  BONDS: U.S. Treasury bond prices rose on generally bond-friendly Federal Reserve speeches Thursday, posting solid gains as investors adjusted positions ahead of the August employment report. \par \par \uc2  Flows were moderate ahead of the bellwether labor report due for release Friday. But speeches by Fed officials, in particular Fed Governor Ben Bernanke and Dallas Fed President Robert McTeer, focusing upon the likelihood that disinflation may linger for another year, pushed Treasurys higher across the board. \par \par \uc2  On a day of speeches by no less than six senior Fed officials, McTeer said, "I doubt there will be a need to fight inflation for quite awhile," and "I believe that further disinflation is more likely over the next year or so than a resurgence of inflation." \par \par \uc2  Bernanke noted that inflation was likely to stay low in the near term, and as a result, the Fed has no need to raise interest rates even though the economy is picking up steam. \par \par \uc2  Such an outcome would "imply that the Fed will keep monetary policy stable for that time frame," said Richard Gilhooly, senior fixed income strategist at BNP Paribas in New York. \par \par \uc2  Drew Matus, an economist at Lehman Brothers in New York said "like Bernanke, we believe that inflation is likely to remain contained and, as a result, the Fed will leave rates unchanged into late 2004. \par \par \uc2  But the week's big number is the labor report Friday. A survey of economists by Dow Jones Newswires predict August payrolls will grow by 12,000, the first positive reading since January. However, the economy needs to generate payroll growth of around 130,000 jobs per month in order to counter a deterioration in the labor market. Meanwhile, the unemployment rate is expected to remain stable at 6.2%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: tokyo Shares Stumble, JGB Prices Gain\par USD-Yen             116.81       gain  0.09    up 0.08%\par AUD-USD             0.6431       gain  0.0003  up 0.06%\par Nikkei 225          10623.43     loss  23.52   dn 0.3%\par Hang Seng           11156.59     gain  17.97   up 0.1%\par JGB Yield           1.4400% down 0.0220\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks inched lower Friday morning as players\par took profits on recent gains in bank shares. Prices of Japanese\par government bonds continued to recover on futures-related buying.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Shares in all four major banking groups - Mizuho, Sumitomo Mitsui,\par UFJ, and Mitsubishi-Tokyo - fell in morning trade. Bank shares had\par climbed sharply earlier in the week on the view the sector was\par progressing with its bad loan disposals and improving its financial\par strength.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par High-technology shares such as Tokyo Electron, a semiconductor\par manufacturing equipment maker, managed to rise in part due to a strong\par sales outlook from the U.S. chip maker Intel Corp., issued just hours\par before the Tokyo market opened.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Japan's three biggest automakers - Toyota, Honda and Nissan - also\par climbed.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Ground Gets Shaky For Shares\par Euro-USD            1.0962      gain  0.0016  up 0.1%\par Stlg-USD            1.5858      gain  0.0038  up 0.3%\par USD-Franc           1.4030      loss  0.0015  dn 0.1%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares may open slightly higher, while\par government bond prices recover on Fed statements that inflation will\par remain low. The euro is holding its higher level.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: European share prices could try to eke out some gains at the\par open, especially tech shares, ahead of the important U.S. labor market\par report at 1230 GMT.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par European stocks were mostly lower Thursday on profit-taking after\par European indexes hit highs for the year the previous session.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein said that questions remain over the\par durability of the current upswing. "We still believe that the recovery\par will be relatively short-lived," it said, highlighting that the U.S.\par labor market remains slack.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Steve Barrow, economist at Bear Stearns, said that while recent\par economic data point to a pick up in the third quarter, he questions\par whether momentum can last into 2004. The key U.S. employment report for\par August will be released and could unsettle market confidence if it\par disappoints.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: Euro-zone government bond prices are expected to rise slightly\par in a continuing recovery from the recent selloff that came on worries\par inflation would pick up with the economic recovery.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Prices rose slightly on Thursday in range trading, despite several\par data releases and the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  press conference.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The market shrugged off the ECB's decision to leave interest rates\par unchanged, as expected, and ignored the ensuing press conference, which\par suggested that rates are likely to be stable for a long time.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Overall, traders said the outlook for the bond market was still\par positive, although range trading is likely to persist over the next few\par weeks.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro starts the session at around $1.0955, gaining slightly\par overnight in the aftermath of massive Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to halt\par the yen's rise early Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sent the euro up about two yen and squeezed out\par investors who were short of the euro. The euro now stands around\par JPY127.95.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Bank of Japan spent an estimated $5 billion to send a clear and\par unequivocal message to the world: they will continue to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to support the dollar as and when they see fit.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Friday, Sep 5: ECB Speakers; U.S. Payrolls\par ET/GMT\par 0100/0500 Japan   July Leading Indicators\par 0600/1000 E.U.    European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Member Solans\par 0830/1230 U.S.    August Non-Farm Payrolls (seen +10K)\par 0830/1230 U.S.    August Unemployment Rate (seen 6.2%)\par 0940/1340 U.S.    August ECRI Inflation Gauge (last 115.4)\par 1130/1530 E.U.    European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Chief Duisenberg\par 1500/1900 U.S.    August Treasury STRIPS\par    N/A    Germany July Industrial Output (seen +0.5%)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181;\par dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Rank Group (U.RNK): 1H Earnings\par Consensus pretax profit before items (Company, 9 analysts): GBP78M\par (GBP85.1M)\par Forecast range: GBP75.8M - GBP80M\par Note: Analysts expecting strength in the provincial casinos, but Hard\par Rock to show the impact of a fall in tourism as a result of the war in\par Iraq.\par RMC (U.RMC): 1H Earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (Company, 10 analysts): GBP53M (GBP81M)\par Note: Analysts looking for evidence of turnaround, especially in hard\par hit German market, to justify share price and optimism on prospects\par for cement price increases. Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein analyst\par Darren Shaw says company "always has the potential to be a turnaround\par situation", but has yet to deliver. Has hold rating on shares, which\par are "very expensive on a p/e basis, but cheap on a takeover basis."\par Go-Ahead Group (U.GOG): FY Earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (Company, 12 analysts): GBP60.7M (GBP49.4M)\par Note: Analyst says market expecting company to write down half the\par balance sheet value, roughly GBP90M, of ground handling business. Also\par looking for outlook on railway franchises, especially Thameslink\par Technip Coflexip (F.TEC): 1H Earnings\par Note: The company said in Jul that 1H orders rose to EUR4B from EUR2.9B\par last year.\par Societe du Louvre (F.LVR): 1H Earnings\par Note: The company said that 1H net profit fell to EUR7.4M from EUR15.6M\par the same period last year.\par Marshalls (U.MSL): 1H Earnings\par Note: The company said in Jul 1H sales rose 7% on a like-for-like basis\par ahead of last year to GBP183M. The company also said it expects to meet\par sales targets for H2.\par Whittard of Chelsea (U.WOC): FY Earnings\par Pretax profit (JCF, 1 analyst): GBP2.2M (GBP1.1M)\par Forecast range: GBP2.2M - GBP2.2M\par Consensus sales: GBP37M (GBP37M)\par Oystertec (U.OYS): 1H Earnings\par Note: The company said in Jun that trading outlook is unchanged since\par 2002 and that it is not expecting sales to match last year's targets.\par JD Wetherspoon (U.WET): FY Earnings\par Taittinger (F.TTG): 1H Earnings\par Aberdeen Intl Fund: EGM\par AGA Foodservices (U.AGA): 1H Earnings\par easyJet (U.EZJ): August traffic\par Apollo, Soros, Cablecom (X.SRS): E.U. antitrust decision\par Coface (F.CFC): 1H Earnings\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 05-09-03 0457GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1011 \