\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar pummeled by jobs data -  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  eyed.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 2003\par 22:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 5 (Reuters) - The dollar fell sharply against its European rivals on Friday after a weaker than expected U.S. jobs report dashed hopes that the economic recovery was on a stronger foundation.  \par \par \uc2 "It is an amazingly bad number ... worse than expected. Treasury bond prices and the interest rate markets are surging in price and dropping yields, so as a result the dollar is going lower across the board," said Tetsuhisa Hayashi, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar and the euro however were well supported against the yen on fears \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to suppress its appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a bid to protect its export sector.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is afraid of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Hayashi.  \par \par \uc2 The August jobs report showed nonfarm payrolls fell 93,000 versus forecasts for a 12,000 rise, while the jobless rate declined to 6.1 percent from 6.2 percent, when no change was expected.  \par \par \uc2 "This ... confirms the jobless recovery is still a viable explanation for what is happening right now," said Michael Woolfolk, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with the Bank of New York.  \par \par \uc2 In late New York trade, the euro surged 1.50 percent to $1.1105 , a two-week high and up from the 3-1/2 month low of $1.0760 on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar slumped to a three-week low of 1.3831 Swiss francs , a loss of 1.45 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling rose to $1.5900 , up 0.5 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The weak jobs report persuaded even more of Wall Street's bond dealers that the Federal Reserve is going to keep rates low for a very long time. Most, though not all, believe the Fed's next move will be to raise interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 In a Reuters poll on Friday, 17 out of 22 primary bond dealers expect the next move will be up sometime in the middle of 2004 or even as late as 2005.  \par \par \uc2 YEN CHECKED BY \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 Fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  are well warranted given traders cited Japanese financial authorities buying dollars for yen on Thursday. That stopped the dollar's decline and catapulting it from 3-1/2 month low of 115.73 yen to back above 116.50 yen.  \par \par \uc2 On Friday, a Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official in New York declined to comment when asked whether the Bank of Japan, acting on behalf of the ministry bought dollars for yen.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar traded at 116.82 yen , up 0.07 percent from Thursday's New York close.  \par \par \uc2 The euro rose to a two-week high of 129.85 yen , up 1.56 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 Traders consulted on Friday had not seen actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but said they had heard that Japanese commercial banks had sold yen against both the dollar and the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "I have not witnessed it, but the price action ... and the underlying bid tone to dollar/yen suggests that Tokyo banks continue to support" the dollar against the yen and the euro against the yen, said Scott Schultz, a trader with Brown Brothers Harriman in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, the yen found support from repeated warnings by U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow against governments' heavy involvement in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but gains were modest with the threat of Japanese yen-selling present in people's minds.  \par \par \uc2 Snow, in Thailand for a meeting of Asian and Pacific policymakers, repeated his concerns that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  be kept to a minimum and that free floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the best economic policy.  \par \par \uc2 "Rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  systems, rather than promoting stability, instead increase the risk that economies cannot properly adjust, thereby escalating the impact of economic shocks," he said. (John Parry and Javier David in New York and James Thornhill in London contributed to this report).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030905dz950050o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
