\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar extends fall on jobs but buoyed vs yen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 554 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 September 2003\par 20:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 5 (Reuters) - The dollar dropped against the euro and the Swiss franc on Friday as traders digested a weaker-than-anticipated U.S. jobs report, signaling apparent deterioration in the labor market of the world's biggest economy.  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar was higher against the yen, leading some New York-based traders to suspect that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been actively engaged in yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 A \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official in New York declined to comment when asked whether the BoJ, acting on behalf of the ministry, had bought dollars for yen on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Traders consulted had not seen actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  but said they had heard that Japanese commercial banks had sold yen against both the dollar and the euro.  \par \par \uc2 "I have not witnessed it, but the price action ... and the underlying bid tone to dollar/yen suggests that Tokyo banks continue to support" the dollar against the yen and the euro against the yen, said Scott Schultz, a trader with Brown Brothers Harriman in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Midafternoon in New York, the euro was steepening its gains and bumping up against the $1.11 area, having already burst above the $1.10 mark soon after the U.S. jobs data were released.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar is really taking it on the chin against major European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and certainly (the employment report) is resonating very badly with the ... market," said Andrew Delano, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with IDEAGlobal in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "The belief is the euro is climbing higher on index-related buying related to the drop in the U.S. dollar index this morning, in the wake of the U.S. data release," said Susan Stearns, vice president of institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales with Bank of Montreal in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The employment report showed that nonfarm payrolls fell by a hefty 93,000, compared with consensus forecasts for a 12,000 rise, while the jobless rate declined to 6.1 percent from 6.2 percent, compared with consensus forecasts for no change.  \par \par \uc2 "This ... confirms the jobless recovery is still a viable explanation for what is happening right now," said Michael Woolfolk, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with the Bank of New York.  \par \par \uc2 Around 1:40 p.m. EDT (1740 GMT), the euro was up 1.4 percent on the day at $1.1097 against the dollar. The dollar was at 116.88 yen against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , up 0.1 percent. Against the Swiss franc , the dollar was at 1.3852 francs, down 1.3 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The pound was at $1.5905, up 0.5 percent.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN, CHINA STANCE REMAINS IN SPOTLIGHT  \par \par \uc2 Earlier, the yen found support from repeated warnings by U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow against governments' heavy involvement in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but gains were modest with the threat of Japanese yen-selling present in people's minds.  \par \par \uc2 Snow, in Thailand for a meeting of Asian and Pacific policymakers, repeated this week's warnings about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the need for more freely floating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the region.  \par \par \uc2 "Rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  systems, rather than promoting stability, instead increase the risk that economies cannot properly adjust, thereby escalating the impact of economic shocks," he said. (Daniel Bases and Javier David in New York and James Thornhill in London contributed to this report).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030905dz95004ti\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
