\par\par \b \uc2  EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Stocks Riding High \b0\par\par\uc2 2,294 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2003\par 07:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par US SUMMARY: Economic Optimism, Cisco Aid Mood\par DJIA          9568.46    gain 45.19     up 0.5%\par NASDAQ        1852.90    gain 11.42     up 0.6%\par S&P 500       1026.27    gain 4.28      up 0.4%\par Dow Future    9557.00    loss 14.00     dn 0.2%\par NASDAQ Future 1362.50    gain 0.50         0.0%\par S&P Future    1025.50    loss 1.50      dn 0.2%\par 10-Yr US Treasury: 4.57% down 0.02\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -(Dow Jones)- Wall Street extended its buying momentum Wednesday as investors grew more optimistic that the economic recovery is firmly on track. Brokerage upgrades of General Electric Co. and Siebel Systems Inc. added to the good mood. Prices of Treasury had small gains. \par \par \uc2  STOCKS: The Dow Jones industrials reached another 14-month high, while the Nasdaq composite and Standard & Poor's 500 index remained at their highest levels in more than a year. \par \par \uc2  "People are back to work and they have some cash on the sidelines that they want to put to work," said John C. Forelli, portfolio manager for Independence Investment LLC. "In the short run, this is a market that wants to go up." \par \par \uc2  "We're continuing to get economic news that's confirming we've got a pretty good recovery unfolding starting in the third quarter, and that's giving investors more confidence," he added. \par \par \uc2  A report on construction spending cheered investors. The Commerce Department said spending rose in July to a seasonally adjusted rate of $879.8 billion. While the 0.2% increase from the previous month wasn't as large as analysts' predictions, the pace was still the highest since January. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. economy grew in most parts of the country, with little ill effect from the August power outage on the East Coast, the Federal Reserve said Wednesday in its so-called Beige Book report. \par \par \uc2  Eleven of the Fed's 12 districts reported improvement during July and August. Back-to-school shopping was "reasonably strong" in eight Fed districts, and the manufacturing sector posted widespread improvements as well. \par \par \uc2  Cisco Systems Inc. Chief Executive John Chambers said orders for the company's computer-networking equipment were "a little bit better than we expected" in August, fueling hopes the embryonic tech recovery may be spreading. \par \par \uc2  BONDS: U.S. Treasurys prices had modest gains in listless trade Wednesday, with the market focusing upon key data later this week. \par \par \uc2  The market displayed little reaction to Wednesday's second-tier economic data and an address on the economy by Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco President Robert Parry. \par \par \uc2  "My view is that the more time we experience growth rates of 4%...and a little better, you'll begin to see the employment picture change. It doesn't suggest you'll see huge changes in unemployment," said Parry, a voting member of the interest rate-setting Federal Open Market Committee. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, smatterings of corporate debt issuance sparked hedging activity in Treasurys and the need for some portfolio managers to make room for new holdings by selling government debt securities. \par \par \uc2  With key data looming, Gerald Lucas, interest rate strategist at Banc of America Securities in New York said, "fundamentals, not positions, should be the primary factor driving prices in the near-term." \par \par \uc2  Aside from several data releases Thursday, led by second quarter productivity and the Institute for Supply Management's non-manufacturing index for August, the session will include speeches by five Fed officials. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Tokyo Shares Higher; USD Jumps On BoJ Buys\par USD-Yen             116.80       gain  0.88    up 0.8%\par AUD-USD             0.6404       gain  0.0006  up 0.08%\par Nikkei 225          10740.31     gain  24.62   up 0.2%\par Hang Seng           11146.13     gain  40.84   up 0.4%\par JGB Yield           1.5350% down 0.0650\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks rose Thursday morning after shares\par gained further on Wall Street amid optimism for U.S. economic growth.\par The dollar jumped against the Japanese yen as Tokyo authorities bought\par the greenback to prevent their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from strengthening, traders\par said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders said the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , under direction from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of\f28 \b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , had repeatedly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market in the\par morning, selling yen for dollars to prevent the yen from gaining too\par fast.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Traders said they spotted the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but the ministry did not\par comment. Kyodo News reported that authorities were selling yen.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the stock market, exporters such as Honda, Toyota, and\par high-technology issues such as NTT DoCoMo and Nintendo rose.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Stocks Next Move Hangs On More Data\par Euro-USD            1.0840      loss  0.0002  dn 0.03%\par Stlg-USD            1.5704      loss  0.0003  dn 0.03%\par USD-Franc           1.4158      gain  0.0001  up 0.01%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares could dip at the open as the\par market reacts to a petering out of strength in U.S. shares late in their\par rally Wednesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Prices of government bonds may rise slightly at the start, while the\par euro holds overnight gains.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par STOCKS: London spreadbettor CityIndex is calling the FTSE and DAX\par slightly lower at 4252 and 3643 respectively. However, the firm said the\par market remains in a very strong phase, although investors may prefer to\par wait for more U.S. economic data to confirm that valuations should go a\par higher still.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par European stocks climbed to year highs Wednesday as investors adopted a\par rosier view on the pace of economic recovery.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Technical factors were also seen driving the market's advance as\par overnight U.S. markets rose beyond recent trading ranges. Liquidity\par returned to pre-holiday levels with a vengeance.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par BONDS: Prices of euro-zone government bonds should open slightly\par higher, with the help of slight recoveries in their Japanese and U.S.\par counterparts. The slightly higher euro should underpin the market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Stronger than expected German Service PMI data pressured the market on\par Wednesday, especially at the front end of the curve.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Data from services PMI demonstrated a strong rise, considering (the\par overall weak situation)," said Antonio Villarroya, analyst with Merrill\par Lynch.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is expected to leave rates unchanged during\par its meeting Thursday.\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro gained slightly on a variety of factors, including\par profit-taking in the dollar and comments from U.S. officials on the\par merits of floating exchange rates.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers also said cited the probe by the office of New York Attorney\par General Eliot Spitzer into alleged illegal trading schemes involving\par four mutual fund companies and a hedge fund. "There was some euro buying\par on those headlines," said the chief dealer at a Japanese bank in New\par York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Looking ahead to Friday's U.S. labor market report for August, dealers\par anticipating a weak number may have been booking profits on recent\par dollar gains ahead of the release. "The dollar has a lot of good news\par priced into it," said Robert Lynch, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at BNP\par Paribas in New York.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par CALENDAR: Thursday, Sep 4: ECB, BoE; US ISM, Fed Speakers\par ET/GMT\par 0300/0700 E.U.     European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Governing Council\par 0400/0800 Germany  August Unemployment\par 0600/1000 U.K.     August Distributive Trades Index\par 0700/1100 U.K.     Bank of England Monetary Policy Committee\par 0745/1145 E.U.     European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate decision\par 0830/1230 E.U.     European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  press conference\par 0830/1230 U.S.     Initial Jobless Claims for August 30 week\par 0830/1230 U.S.     2Q Productivity and Costs\par 1000/1400 U.S.     August ISM Non-Manufacturing Report\par 1000/1400 U.S.     DJ-BTM Business Barometer for August 23\par 1000/1400 U.S.     July Factory Orders (seen +0.8%)\par 1200/1600 U.S.     Federal Reserve Governor Gramlich speaks\par 1215/1615 U.S.     Federal Reserve Governor Bernanke\par 1330/1730 U.S.     Dallas Fed Pres McTeer speaks\par 1500/1900 U.S.     San Francisco Fed Pres Parry addresses\par 1630/2030 U.S.     Money Supply\par 1830/2230 U.S.     St. Louis Fed Pres Poole\par    N/A    U.K.     British Retail Confederation Shop Price\par    N/A    Germany  July Manufacturing Orders (seen -0.4%)\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; 41-1-212-2181;\par dennis.baker@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Diageo (U.DEO): FY Earnings\par Consensus pretax profit (DJ, 3 analysts): GBP2.13B (GBP2.04B)\par Note: Analysts say the performance of Bailey's, Smirnoff and Johnnie\par Walker Black may offer encouragement, while expected flat volume\par performance from Guinness for the second year in a row, is of some\par concern.\par Royal & Sun Alliance (U.RSA): 1H Earnings\par Consensus operating profit (Company, 13 analysts): GBP354M (GBP301M)\par Forecast range: GBP327M - GBP386M\par Note: Interim div seen at 2.6p vs 4p last year. Analysts looking for\par confirmation of company's rights issue plans, expected between GBP700M\par and GBP1B. But say benign weather and rate increases should lead to\par higher 1H profits.\par Suez (F.SZE): 1H Earnings\par Consensus net loss (DJ, 5 analysts): EUR118M (EUR164M profit)\par Note: Suez has warned that it would book a "substantial" charge in the\par first half to account for assets it sold at a loss. Analysts expect\par Suez's earnings before interest, taxes depreciation and amortization\par to fall 15% to EUR3.3B from EUR3.9B a year earlier. Company is in\par midst of a two-year effort to narrow the focus of its businesses and\par lower its debt.\par Pinault Printemps Redoute (F.PPR): 1H Earnings\par Consensus operating profit (DJ, 6 analysts): EUR588M (EUR834M)\par Note: With one-offs unknown, estimates for net vary from EUR63M to\par EUR137M, down from EUR283M last time. Points to look for: guidance on\par performance at Gucci and Rexel.\par Casino (F.CGD): 1H Earnings\par Consensus net profit (DJ, 4 analysts): EUR198M (EUR178M)\par Note: With France providing 95% of operating profit, home territory\par performance again likely to be scrutinized. But international\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also of note,