\par\par \b \uc2  MONUMENT SECURITIES: An Asian Locomotive \b0\par\par\uc2  By Stephen Lewis \par \uc2 1,044 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2003\par 13:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of Monument Securities \par \par \uc2  LONDON -(Dow Jones)- With Mr Snow safely on his way to the APEC conference and Tokyo equity prices running out of momentum, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BoJ) took the opportunity to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign exchanges. It is reported to have bought the US dollar when that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was trading at 115.75 against the yen.  The implication is that Japanese exchange rate policy is unchanged and that there is little to be gained from taking on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  So far, the US Treasury Secretary has little to show for his tour of the Far East. However, he is likely to return, at the APEC meeting today, to his arguments in favour of flexible exchange rates and the removal of capital controls. The latter objective is especially important since its achievement would allow US financial interests to participate more fully in Asian growth. \par \par \uc2  Further evidence of the buoyancy of Asian economies came today with the release of Japanese companies' capital spending figures for 2003Q2. These showed a 6.4% rise, year on year. In seven quarters, this was the first to record an annual increase. This fact suggests that a corner has been turned. The recovery in Japanese corporate profits, partly derived from exports and overseas sales, seems to be fuelling an investment-led recovery in business activity.  The prospect that a Japanese economic revival will combine with sustained strength in Chinese production to pull the global economy out of the mire carries important implications for the rest of the world. \par \par \uc2  An Asian growth locomotive would probably operate in a different way from the US locomotive that has usually pulled along global growth in recent decades. Expansion in the US economy has stimulated business activity elsewhere typically through two channels.  First, a US economic upswing has been accompanied by increased demand for imports of a wide range of goods and services. Other countries' economies have benefited as they have met this demand. Secondly, stronger US domestic growth has boosted profits of US companies, leaving them with more funds to invest at home and abroad.  To the extent that US companies have committed more money to investment abroad, they have contributed to raising the level of demand in foreign countries. With Asia leading the way, things are likely to be different.  It would be unfair to claim that Asian economies, when they expand, do not benefit the rest of the world through trade channels.  Both Japan and China are running substantial trade surpluses but China, at least, is seeing import growth more than commensurate with the rate of expansion of its GDP.  The latest statistics show that in the first seven months of 2003, Chinese imports, in value terms, rose by 42% from their level in the corresponding period in 2002.  However, imports into China, and into other newly developed Asian economies, are skewed more heavily towards capital goods than are those of the USA.  Consequently, the countries that are likely to respond most immediately to the pull of the Asian locomotive are those that produce the capital goods that Asian economies need. Chinese companies generally have not yet reached a stage of development where they are tempted to deploy funds in foreign ventures, though Japanese companies would probably do so. Overall, however, the investment channel for the transmission of growth to the rest of the world is likely to be weaker with the Asian locomotive than with the US.  At the same time, Asian growth could well give an even stronger boost to the market prices of raw materials than does US growth, given the relatively poor state of development of natural resources in the Asian region and its dependence on the global market for marginal supplies. Accordingly, copper is currently trading in the London market at a price some 20% higher than a year ago.  If the Asian business upswing persists, global demand for industrial materials can be expected to increase further, putting more upward pressure on prices.  This would be good news for the suppliers of these raw materials.  They would be among the first beneficiaries outside Asia of an Asian-led world recovery whereas they tend to be at the tail-end of the cycle when the USA is driving the upswing. \par \par \uc2  These considerations may appear academic to those who look to the US economy to expand strongly and to pace global growth as usual. There have been some impressive figures from the US economy in recent weeks. Some of them are not as good as they seem, however. Auto sales data released yesterday, for example, were better news for Japan than for the USA. In August, US domestic vehicle sales were 2.6% down on the previous year. The Big Three manufacturers all dropped sales.  Three Japanese companies, Toyota, Honda and Nissan, on the other hand, all recorded double-figure year-on-year gains in unit sales.  Though some 70% of Japanese car sales in the USA are supplied from US plants, there may still have been some leakage of US demand into imports.  This needs to be borne in mind in judging the GDP impact of the auto sales numbers. There is also likely to be a GDP offset from a rundown of previously bloated auto inventories.  Despite August's buoyant demand conditions, Ford confirmed its plans to cut production. \par \par \uc2  -By Stephen Lewis: 44 20 7338 0179: analysismonumentsecurities.com \par \par \uc2  (Stephen Lewis is chief economist at Monument Securities Ltd., London, independent brokers specializing in institutional business.) \par \par \uc2  Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires. \par \par \uc2  This column is published for information only, and it neither constitutes, nor is to be construed as, an offer to buy or sell investments. The information and opinions expressed herein are based on sources the author believes to be reliable, but he cannot represent that they are accurate or complete. Any information herein is given in good faith, but is subject to change without notice. No liability is accepted whatsoever by Monument Securities Ltd., employees and associated companies for any direct or consequential loss arising from this article. Monument Securities Ltd. is regulated by the SFA and is a member of the London Stock Exchange, LIFFE and ISMA. \par \par \uc2  1089 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020030904dz94002mh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
