\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of Japan Supports Dollar Against Yen Amid Selloff \b0\par\par\uc2  By Tom Barkley and Jamie McGeever \par \uc2 875 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2003\par 22:41\par \uc2  Dow Jones Business News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2003, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Dow Jones Newswires \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK -- The dollar sailed high and steady against the yen Thursday on what traders attributed to a heavy dose of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities. \par \par \uc2  But the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sagged against other major counterparts on growing concerns that the country is in the midst of a jobless recovery, following an unexpected pickup in first-time applications for unemployment benefits last week. \par \par \uc2  Or more accurately, it appears to be a "job-loss" recovery, since that is how Federal Reserve Governor Ben Bernanke described the state of the economy Thursday following a speech in New York. \par \par \uc2  "I would argue that, in situations of considerable slack, growth that is generated solely by increased productivity, and that is unaccompanied by substantial employment growth, may possibly require monetary ease, rather than monetary tightening, in the short run," Mr. Bernanke said in his speech. \par \par \uc2  He said the Fed may need to lower interest rates further if faster economic growth isn't accompanied by rising employment, adding that "soft labor markets and excess capacity create a further downward risk to inflation." \par \par \uc2  However, Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas President Robert McTeer, who also warned of "disinflation" Thursday in a speech in Dallas, said that a growing U.S. economy should stimulate job creation even though worker productivity continues to improve. \par \par \uc2  Mr. McTeer later added that the unemployment rate could fall as soon as Friday, when the August payroll data is released. \par \par \uc2  The much-awaited monthly employment data came into even sharper focus Thursday after an unexpected rise in weekly jobless claims. On a day filled with U.S. data and appearances by Fed officials, the figure that stood out the most was the 15,000 jump in initial jobless claims to 413,000 in the week ended Aug. 30, its highest level since the week ended July 12. \par \par \uc2  Economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires and CNBC expect payrolls to increase by 12,000 in August after six straight monthly declines, and for the jobless rate to be unchanged at 6.2%. \par \par \uc2  However, Paul Podolsky, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Fleet Bank in Boston, said payrolls would have to move 60,000 or more in either direction to move the market. \par \par \uc2  While he views the jobless claims numbers as possibly a catalyst for the euro's gains Thursday, the main driver was the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which sent the euro up about two yen and squeezed out investors who were short the euro. \par \par \uc2  Thursday afternoon, the euro was trading at its intraday high around $1.0944, up from $1.0836 late Wednesday in New York. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also trading around 127.76 yen, up from 125.69 yen. \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 116.73 yen, up from 115.97 yen late Wednesday in New York. Elsewhere, the dollar was quoted around 1.4062 francs, down from 1.4157 francs, while sterling was trading at $1.5812, up from $1.5705. \par \par \uc2  The BOJ's massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's appreciation - which according to several dealers appeared to be in excess of $5 billion - sent a clear and unequivocal message to the world: they will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to support the dollar as and when they see fit. \par \par \uc2  While that may seem surprising given that U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow is still in Asia spreading the gospel on flexible exchange-rate policy. The U.S. is much more concerned with China than Japan. \par \par \uc2  Phil Suttle, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at JP Morgan in New York, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is "consistent with Snow's message ... and doesn't undermine anything he's saying." \par \par \uc2  If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  helps sustain a nascent export-led economic recovery, then so be it, as a recovery in the world's second largest economy is in everyone's interest, including the U.S. And as \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  invariably involves the purchase of U.S. Treasurys, it helps finance the U.S. massive current account deficit. \par \par \uc2  For the U.S., "the desire is not to undercut or undermine the potential for Japan to grow again," said Alan Ruskin, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at independent researchers 4Cast, noting that previous attempts at a recovery in Japan over the last 12 years have been snuffed out by various external shocks. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere Thursday, announcements by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Bank of England that interest rates were left unchanged had been expected and didn't have much impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  However, the dollar fell to its lowest level against the Canadian dollar in nearly two months. The two-day slide began Wednesday, when the Bank of Canada cut it benchmark interest rate by 25 basis points to 2.75%, but surprised market players with a statement that strongly signaled there will be no more interest rate cuts from Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Late Thursday, the dollar was trading at C$1.3700, down from C$1.3794 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385\par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com\par -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096;\par     jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 09-04-03 1641ET\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020030904dz94005eh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
