\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slips vs euro after US jobless data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 706 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2003\par 15:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comments, changes byline, dateline, previous LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 4 (Reuters) - The dollar slipped against the euro in the wake of unexpectedly weak U.S. jobless claims data on Thursday but moved higher against the yen, which remained the main mover amongst the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The yen fell 1 percent from a 3-1/2 month high against the dollar after Japanese authorities apparently sold yen heavily during the Asian trading session in a renewed drive to protect exporters.  \par \par \uc2 Traders in New York were on the lookout for any more yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with some expecting the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars not far below current levels.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  one day on, one day off is not going to work. You have to be at least consistent about it," said Thomas Molloy, a trader with Bank Leumi in New York, who expects the BoJ might step in to buy dollars at the 116.40 yen to 116.50 yen support area.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities "have avoided a potentially more serious breakdown (of the dollar against the yen) but I doubt they are particularly pleased with these kind of levels. The question is how aggressive they would want to be," said Robert Sinche, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research with Citibank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The BoJ may have sold between $2 billion and $3 billion-worth of yen, catching the market off guard, Molloy said.  \par \par \uc2 Japan gave no confirmation whether it had stepped in but Tokyo traders saw the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which conducts \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the Finance Ministry, selling yen and pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down more than one yen.  \par \par \uc2 JOBLESS RECOVERY IRKS DOLLAR  \par \par \uc2 Early in the U.S. session, the U.S. Labor Department reported jobless claims rose to 413,000 in the week of Aug. 30 from a revised 398,000 the prior week, overshooting consensus expectations for 390,000. The Labor Department also reported that U.S. second quarter productivity growth was revised up to 6.8 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a continual theme of a jobless recovery. Productivity gains are above expectations," said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Investors Bank & Trust in Boston. "Unemployment remains high, but the people who are working are extremely productive," and the reminder of this is weighing on the dollar, he said.  \par \par \uc2 The claims numbers may dull the growing perception of nascent employment growth ahead of the hotly-anticipated August U.S. employment report for release on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Among Thursday's U.S. economic reports, traders are now focusing on the Institute for Supply Management's nonmanufacturing index, and factory orders, for release at 10a.m. EDT (1400 GMT). The ISM index is expected to fall to 63.0 from 65.1 in July.  \par \par \uc2 A bevy of officials from the Federal Reserve may offer more clues to the state of the U.S. economic recovery when they give speeches in separate events due in New York trading hours.  \par \par \uc2 The euro retreated modestly after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held its minimum bid rate at 2.0 percent. The ECB was not expected to change borrowing costs, which are now at a record low of 2 percent. The Bank of England left key interest rates at 3.5 percent, as expected.  \par \par \uc2 Around 9:30 a.m. EDT (1330 GMT), the euro was at $1.0879 against the dollar, up 0.3 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was at 116.99 yen against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , up 0.99 percent and more than a full yen above its session low of 115.78 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at 127.29 yen , up 1.3 percent on the day and well above its 7-1/2 month lows plumbed on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc the dollar was at 1.4139 francs, down 0.06 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 The yen action came as market speculation grew Japan might become less aggressive in holding the yen down due either to domestic economic recovery or pressure from Washington not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively. Such pressure was reinforced by U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow in a visit this week. (Justina Pawlak in London contributed to this report).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030905dz940019s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
