\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar drops on job report jitters.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Daniel Bases  \par \uc2 683 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2003\par 23:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 4 (Reuters) - The dollar fell broadly on Thursday after a poor weekly jobs report spooked investors ahead of Friday's important August employment data which is critical in determining the health of the U.S. recovery.  \par \par \uc2 However the dollar and euro gained against the yen due to what traders said was yen selling by the Japanese government aimed at weakening its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to protect its export sector. Japanese officials would not confirm any actions.  \par \par \uc2 The weakness in the dollar came after first-time claims for unemployment benefits rose above 400,000, an important level that indicates weakness in job growth. Claims rose by 15,000 to 413,000 in the week ended Aug. 30 - above economists' expectations for a small decline to 390,000.  \par \par \uc2 The claims numbers may dull hopes that Friday's hotly anticipated August U.S. employment report will bring good news for Americans looking for jobs.  \par \par \uc2 "There has been a little bit of a switch, a softer tone in the dollar across the board. I think it does suggest the market was quite sensitive to any piece of data that might interrupt the happy stream of news that had been present in the economic data," said Jason Bonanca, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at Credit Suisse First Boston in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Some views on the employment front from Federal Reserve officials were restrained on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Dallas Federal Reserve Bank President Robert McTeer said that economic growth in the third quarter should be strong enough for some job creation, while Fed Governor Ben Bernanke said a decline in the U.S. jobless rate, though steady, is "likely to be slow."  \par \par \uc2 Given that employment is viewed as the last critical piece of the U.S. recovery, the market shrugged off a slew of other strong economic reports on Thursday highlighting second-quarter productivity, August service sector growth and July factory orders.  \par \par \uc2 Economists expect Friday's employment report to show an increase of 12,000 jobs and a steady employment rate of 6.2 percent, the latest Reuters survey found.  \par \par \uc2 The euro rose to $1.0944, up 0.92 percent on the day and rising from a low of $1.0810.  \par \par \uc2 The euro jumped 1.64 percent against the yen, well above the 7-1/2-month lows plumbed on Wednesday, to trade at 127.76 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also rose against the yen, trading at 116.70 yen, a gain of 0.74 percent on the day and up from the fresh 3-1/2-month low of 115.78 yen hit prior to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders cited.  \par \par \uc2 The euro retreated modestly after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as expected, held its minimum bid rate at 2 percent. The Bank of England left key interest rates at 3.5 percent, also as expected. Sterling rose to $1.5814, up 0.64 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 JAPANESE \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 All signs in the market pointed to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday, a day after U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow left Japan for Phuket, Thailand and a meeting of the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) forum.  \par \par \uc2 In Phuket, Snow repeated what he had said in Tokyo: That \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values are best established in open and competitive markets with \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept to a minimum.  \par \par \uc2 "It is quite clear (the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's) strategy is to keep the (dollar) up and their tactics have been quite impressive. This day though is really quite hilarious - as the Secretary's plane was fading into the horizon, one imagines the MOF asking if his plane has taken off yet before getting into the market," said CSFB's Bonanca.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan may have sold between $2 billion and $3 billion worth of yen, catching the market off guard, said Tommy Molloy, trader at Bank Leumi in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Japan gave no confirmation whether it had stepped in but Tokyo traders saw the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which conducts \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the Finance Ministry, selling yen and pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down more than one yen against the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030904dz940059e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
