\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar down vs euro as U.S. data fail to help.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By John Parry  \par \uc2 696 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 September 2003\par 17:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Sept 4 (Reuters) - The dollar slipped against the euro, digesting unexpectedly weak U.S. jobless claims data on Thursday, but was sharply higher against the yen, which remained the main mover amongst the major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Stronger-than-expected U.S. service sector and factory orders reports failed to boost the greenback much, which drew only a fleeting bid from these data, as traders brooded on the upcoming monthly employment report for release on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The Institute of Supply Management (ISM) reported its U.S. non-manufacturing or service sector activity index was unchanged in August at 65.1, compared to July. Meanwhile, the U.S. Commerce Department reported July factory orders rose 1.6 percent. Both reports were stronger than consensus forecasts.  \par \par \uc2 "The ISM numbers are a little better than expected. There were some expectations of a little giveback in the numbers. It's mildly positive for the dollar. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market needs to be fed firmer numbers," said Tim Stewart, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Morgan Stanley in New York.  \par \par \uc2 In Tokyo, the yen fell 1 percent from a 3-1/2 month high against the dollar after Japanese authorities apparently sold yen heavily during the Asian trading session in a renewed drive to protect exporters.  \par \par \uc2 Traders in New York were on the lookout for any more yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with some expecting the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy dollars not far below current levels.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  one day on, one day off is not going to work. You have to be at least consistent about it," said Thomas Molloy, a trader with Bank Leumi in New York, who expects the BoJ might step in to buy dollars at the 116.40 yen to 116.50 yen support area.  \par \par \uc2 The BoJ may have sold between $2 billion and $3 billion-worth of yen, catching the market off guard, Molloy said.  \par \par \uc2 Japan gave no confirmation whether it had stepped in but Tokyo traders saw the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which conducts \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the Finance Ministry, selling yen and pushing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down more than one yen.  \par \par \uc2 JOBLESS RECOVERY IRKS DOLLAR  \par \par \uc2 Early in the U.S. session, the U.S. Labor Department reported jobless claims rose to 413,000 in the week of Aug. 30 from a revised 398,000 the prior week, overshooting consensus expectations for 390,000. The Labor Department also reported that U.S. second quarter productivity growth was revised up to 6.8 percent.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a continual theme of a jobless recovery. Productivity gains are above expectations," said Tim Mazanec, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with Investors Bank & Trust in Boston. "Unemployment remains high, but the people who are working are extremely productive," and the reminder of this is weighing on the dollar, he said.  \par \par \uc2 The claims numbers may dull the growing perception of nascent employment growth ahead of the hotly-anticipated August U.S. employment report for release on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 A bevy of officials from the Federal Reserve may offer more clues to the state of the U.S. economic recovery as they give speeches in separate events due in New York trading hours.  \par \par \uc2 The euro retreated modestly after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held its minimum bid rate at 2.0 percent. The ECB was not expected to change borrowing costs, which are now at a record low of 2 percent. The Bank of England left key interest rates at 3.5 percent, as expected.  \par \par \uc2 Around 10:33 a.m. EDT (1433 GMT), the euro was at $1.0881 against the dollar, up 0.3 percent on the day.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was at 116.80 yen against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , up 0.8 percent and a full yen above its session low of 115.78 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at 127.14 yen , up 1.1 percent on the day and well above its 7-1/2 month lows plumbed on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc the dollar was at 1.4137 francs, down 0.07 percent on the day. (Gertrude Chavez in New York and Justina Pawlak in London contributed to this report).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020030904dz940045b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
